{
  "raw": {
    "https://smithcollege.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/smithcollege/job/Smith-College/Manager-of-Facilities--Evening-Operations_R-202600626": {
      "title": "Manager of Facilities, Evening Operations",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-30\nPosted: Posted 14 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nIf you have any questions about the position or our application process, reach out to us at recruiting-u@smith.edu . Department Facilities Management Job Description Job Summary Assist with the management and execution of the department’s custodial operations, with direct responsibility for supervising evening custodial staff and supplemental oversight of evening event staff who report to a day-shift manager. Ensure that assigned staff receive appropriate direction, support, and oversight to maintain service standards and operational continuity throughout the evening shift. This includes scheduling, evaluating, and training staff and student workers. Manage facilities operations during evening shift hours. Make recommendations for and work closely with vendors to obtain cost estimates for furnishings, supplies and equipment. Foster a climate of growth and leadership among staff while maintaining cooperative relationships with colleagues and other campus departments. Coordinate and conduct training sessions to uphold consistent standards across divisions. Take appropriate actions to support a diverse workforce and participate in the College's efforts to create a respectful, inclusive, and welcoming work environment. Essential Functions Custodial Operations (35%): Coordinate with the Associate Director of Facilities Operations to manage evening custodial operations and event support, ensuring appropriate staffing, communication, and service delivery across assigned facilities. Manage day-to-day Facilities operations during the evening shift, serving as the primary point of contact for Campus Safety and other campus partners for facility-related needs, emergencies, and operational concerns. Respond to emergency calls and facility-related incidents during the evening shift. Assess situations, determine appropriate corrective actions, and coordinate the deployment of Facilities on-call personnel, custodial staff, and other resources as needed. Coordinate custodial support for campus events and special activities, ensuring staffing and service requirements are communicated clearly and completed in accordance with established expectations. Research, evaluate, and recommend appropriate furnishings, custodial supplies, equipment, and operational resources to support effective and efficient service delivery throughout assigned areas. Collaborate with the supervisor of the Grounds Department to ensure campus readiness during inclement weather and other weather-related conditions, including coordinating and scheduling custodial staff support as needed. Monitor evening custodial operations to ensure established service standards, departmental procedures, safety requirements, and customer service expectations are consistently maintained. Communicate operational issues, staffing needs, service concerns, and significant incidents to the Associate Director of Facilities Operations and other appropriate department leadership in a timely manner. Custodial Supervision (35%): Supervise, train, coach, and motivate custodial staff to ensure work is performed effectively, efficiently, safely, and in accordance with departmental standards, policies, and procedures. Conduct regular meetings with Coordinating Custodians and custodial staff to communicate operational plans, review policies and procedures, address service needs, and promote teamwork, accountability, and a clear understanding of departmental expectations and work rules. Conduct regular inspections of assigned academic and residential facilities to assess cleanliness, service levels, safety, and overall condition. Follow up on identified concerns and ensure appropriate corrective action is taken. Document building inspections, service concerns, and follow-up activities. Work collaboratively with employees to identify areas for improvement, establish performance expectations and goals, and monitor progress. Evaluate and document custodial performance and work with Manager on progressive discipline if warranted. Interview job applicants and recommend new hires. Encourage problem solving and create appropriate independent customer service skills within work group. Administration (20%): Review and approve custodial payroll. Oversee work order process, weekly staff reports, distribute and track overtime for custodial coverage, building checks, event support and additional projects when assigned. Investigate, document, and prepare billing for all damages in academic buildings. Maintain comprehensive knowledge of current union contract(s) and comply with all contract language. Participate in and contribute to department and management meetings. Training (10%): Contribute to the design and implementation of departmental training programs. Schedule and conduct training sessions to ensure a high level of safety and cleanliness standards are upheld across divisions. Attend educational opportunities aimed at enhancing professional skills.   Perform other duties as assigned by management. MINIMUM REQUIRED Qualifications: Bachelor's degree 5 years of relevant experience with at least 1 year of leadership and/or a combination of education and experience. Knowledge and experience in residential and institutional operations. Skills: Excellent verbal and written communication ability and customer service skills. Demonstrated ability to work with students, student organizations, faculty, and staff. Ability to organize and prioritize work, takes initiative, resolve problems, and manage teams. Must work well independently and manage multiple tasks simultaneously. Must be proficient in PC systems and software. Ability to work a flexible schedule, which includes days, evenings and weekends. Preferred Certification(s): Must maintain current OSA Blood-borne Pathogen training certification. Compensation In addition to a competitive benefits package, annualized pay for this role is typically between $63,029.35–$76,535.64 covering the middle half of market compensation. Actual compensation will vary based on individual qualifications and work schedule if less than full-time. Position Type Regular Please attach BOTH a current resume and a cover letter in order for your application to be considered for this position. Be sure you have provided all attachments before submitting your application. You will NOT be able to attach additional files after you have hit the Submit button. Review of applications will begin About Smith College Located in Northampton, MA, Smith College is one of the largest women’s colleges in the country and is dedicated to excellence in teaching and research across the liberal arts.  A faculty of outstanding scholars interact with students in small classes, as advisors, and through student-faculty research projects. The College is a member of the Five College Consortium with Amherst, Hampshire and Mt. Holyoke Colleges, and the University of Massachusetts Amherst. Students cross-enroll and faculty cross-teach across the Five Colleges. Consistent with the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) and Massachusetts General Law, Chapter 151B, it is the policy of Smith College to provide reasonable accommodation when requested by a qualified applicant or employee with a disability, unless such accommodation would cause an undue hardship. The policy regarding requests for reasonable accommodation applies to all aspects of employment, including the application process. If reasonable accommodation is needed, please contact our recruiting team at recruiting-u@smith.edu . As set forth in our mission and values , Smith College is committed to promoting a culture of  equity and inclusion among students, staff and faculty.  The College will not discriminate in employment on the basis of age, race, color, ethnicity, national origin, creed, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, pregnancy, genetic information, age, veteran status, physical or mental disability, or any other classification protected by law. Smith College is an equal opportunity employer and complies with all state and federal laws that prohibit discrimination."
    },
    "https://smithcollege.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/smithcollege/job/Smith-College/Science-Center-Senior-Director_R-202600523": {
      "title": "Science Center Senior Director",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-01\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nIf you have any questions about the position or our application process, reach out to us at recruiting-u@smith.edu . Department Clark Science Center Administration Job Description Job Summary Ensure excellence of Clark Science Center operations and planning. Promote teamwork and professional development in the delivery of administrative and technical services to the science community. Develop working practices and relationships to ensure equitable financial management and sustainable resources for 20 Science, Technology, Engineering & Math (STEM) departments, programs and centers.  Responsible for the day-to-day management of the Clark Science Center, which includes facilities and budget management, and oversight of around 30 staff positions in nine administrative and technical units servicing twenty academic departments and programs housed in a quarter of a million square feet of assignable space in five buildings and a portion of Scott Gym. Take appropriate actions to support a diverse workforce and participate in the College's efforts to create a respectful, inclusive, and welcoming work environment. Essential Functions Administration (40%): Oversee the effective and efficient operations and administration of the Clark Science Center. Oversee the support provided by the Academic Admin coordinator team and integrate with the needs of the science departments and programs, Computing & Technical Support, Resource Management/Stockrooms, Animal Care Facility, Center for Molecular Biology and Aqueous Biogeochemistry,  Center for Mass Spectrometry, Center for Design & Fabrication, Lab Safety & Compliance, and the Institutional Animal Care & Use Committee staff. Ensure the integration of Science Center units to deliver comprehensive, coordinated services.  Act as the chair of certain standing committees and serve on others; including new committees that may be needed in the future. Carry out the hiring, supervision, and evaluation of support staff, both clerical and technical. Oversee the maintenance of regulatory licenses for inventories of controlled substances and radionuclides. Responsible for insuring that the Science Center is in compliance with local, state, and federal regulations regarding health and safety, and toxic substances. Administer grants secured for the sciences when individual faculty members, departments, or other administrative units are not the appropriate administrators. Allocate summer student research funding and collaborate on admission and assessment efforts.  Review, negotiate and execute Material Transfer Agreements, ensuring the College’s and faculty interests and responsibilities are consistent with academic norms, policy and risk management standards.  Facilities Planning and Management (30%): Develop annual and long-term plans to renovate space to meet evolving needs.  Partner with Facilities, Capital Projects, architects and space occupants to translate plans into construction documents that are aligned with financial constraints.  Serve as Science Center’s and occupants’ representative during construction. Oversee security, safety, animal care facilities, supervision of the maintenance of shared equipment and facilities that are not the specific responsibility of academic departments or of Facilities Management. Oversee and, when necessary, make recommendations to the Office of the Provost and Dean of Faculty regarding the allocation of non-classroom Science Center space. Program Planning and Development (20%): Prepare long and short-term plans for the development of programs and services in the sciences, in cooperation with the Science Planning Committee and members of the science faculty. Coordinate with Alumnae Relations and Development and other stakeholders on strategic fundraising opportunities to support the sciences. In partnership with the Faculty Director, initiate and draft grant applications. Visit granting agencies. Seek out sources of potential funding. Serve as an effective spokesperson for the sciences. Serve as a two-way communications link to numerous other campus constituencies. Budget Management and Planning (10%): Manage the operating, endowed, and grant budgets totaling over $5 million including the Science Center’s Equipment Capital Plan. Management/Leadership: Manages a team of direct reports in regular staff roles Accountable for hiring, development / coaching, and performance-related decisions with support from Human Resources Makes informed decisions impacting team and college including setting priorities, allocating resources, and problem-solving Effectively communicates expectations, provides feedback, and ensures alignment with department goals Addresses conflicts and challenges within the team to promote a positive work environment Formulates and implements policies and procedures  MINIMUM REQUIRED Qualifications: Graduate Degree, Field:  Business Administration, Science, Educational Administration or related field 12 or more years experience, Area: Managing people, managing complex, multifaceted operations, multi-million dollar budgets, managing or using scientific equipment Skills: Excellent interpersonal, problem solving, and written/oral communication skills. Ability to interact with a wide range of people, involving a diverse array of topics, in a fast-paced environment. Must have expense tracking, budget management and planning experience. Proficient in Google Apps and Microsoft Office applications, including Word, Excel, and PowerPoint, as well as database user skills (e.g. MS Access or FileMaker). Familiarity with online communication preferred (e.g. web site, blog, YouTube). Compensation In addition to a competitive benefits package, annualized pay for this role is typically between $105,505.22 - $142,742.36 covering the middle half of market compensation. Actual compensation will vary based on individual qualifications and work schedule if less than full-time. Position Type Regular Please attach BOTH a current resume and a cover letter in order for your application to be considered for this position . Be sure you have provided all attachments before submitting your application. You will NOT be able to attach additional files after you have hit the Submit button. Review of applications will begin October 14, 2026 About Smith College Located in Northampton, MA, Smith College is one of the largest women’s colleges in the country and is dedicated to excellence in teaching and research across the liberal arts.  A faculty of outstanding scholars interact with students in small classes, as advisors, and through student-faculty research projects. The College is a member of the Five College Consortium with Amherst, Hampshire and Mt. Holyoke Colleges, and the University of Massachusetts Amherst.  Students cross-enroll and faculty cross-teach across the Five Colleges. Consistent with the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) and Massachusetts General Law, Chapter 151B, it is the policy of Smith College to provide reasonable accommodation when requested by a qualified applicant or employee with a disability, unless such accommodation would cause an undue hardship. The policy regarding requests for reasonable accommodation applies to all aspects of employment, including the application process. If reasonable accommodation is needed, please contact our recruiting team at recruiting-u@smith.edu . As set forth in our mission and values , Smith College is committed to promoting a culture of  equity and inclusion among students, staff and faculty.  The College will not discriminate in employment on the basis of age, race, color, ethnicity, national origin, creed, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, pregnancy, genetic information, age, veteran status, physical or mental disability, or any other classification protected by law. Smith College is an equal opportunity employer and complies with all state and federal laws that prohibit discrimination."
    },
    "https://smithcollege.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/smithcollege/job/Smith-College/Collections-Registrar_R-202600323": {
      "title": "Collections Registrar",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-07\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nIf you have any questions about the position or our application process, reach out to us at recruiting-u@smith.edu . Department Museum of Art Job Description Job Summary As a member of the Collections Management team at the Smith College Museum of Art, the Associate Registrar plays a key role in the stewardship, documentation, and accessibility of the museum’s collection. The Associate Registrar holds primary responsibility for the administration of the museum’s collections database, manages rights and reproductions, and oversees collections digitization. They are responsible for complying with applicable laws, regulations, and professional standards related to collections management, documentation, intellectual property, and the stewardship of culturally sensitive material. Working both independently and collaboratively, they establish and maintain systems that support the collections care, use, and discovery. The Associate Registrar also serves as a critical partner in collections and building management projects and coordinating other essential collections-related activities. Take appropriate actions to support a diverse workforce and participate in the College's efforts to create a respectful, inclusive, and welcoming work environment. Essential Functions Collections Access and Location Control (30%): Facilitate collection access for in-person visits by students, classes, and academic researchers Manage location control for SCMA’s collection, overseeing annual inventories for insurance compliance as well as confirming the presence and condition of artworks Oversee Cunningham Center location management in coordination with the Cunningham Center Manager Direct organization of art storage areas to maximize efficient use of storage space Oversee collections objects’ location control during moves including major building projects and renovations Database Administration (30%) Manage SCMA’s implementation of the consortial Five College Collections Management System (Qi/Keepthinking) Provide training to SCMA staff and other Smith College stakeholders in both the Qi CMS as well as the Qi public portal Liaise with FCI and Keepthinking to resolve database issues and implement software updates Serve as SCMA liaison to the consortial Collections Management and Access Committee which sets standards for data governance Collections Documentation (10%): Manage the digitization of the collection including object photography and critical object records   Maintain confidential paper files relating to the collection in conjunction with the digital files in the Collections Management System Create acquisition paperwork and documentation. Create and manage workflows for intake of artworks. Exhibitions (10%): Assist with logistics, paperwork, condition reporting, and installation/deinstallation of exhibitions as needed Work with Exhibitions and Loans Specialist to ensure exhibitions documentation is entered into the Collections Management System in a consistent, timely manner Rights and reproductions (10%) Oversee external rights and reproductions requests and fulfilment for permanent collections objects Manage copyright permissions and image use for SCMA Update Collections Management System with rights and reproductions information and work with artists and artist representatives to gain copyright permissions for new acquisitions Collections Stewardship (10%) Oversee processing and disposition of found-in-collections materials, complying with appropriate abandoned property laws Oversee processing of large, previously unprocessed gifts Oversee collections logistics of deaccessioning artworks identified through curatorial assessment, including provenance and title research   Perform other duties as assigned by management. MINIMUM REQUIRED Qualifications: Graduate Degree, Field: Museum studies, MLIS, or related 5 or more years experience, Area: Museum Collections Management or Registration   Knowledge of: Museum core standards and best practices for Collections Management, relational databases, museum lexica including Getty AAT, legal regulations governing abandoned property, title and provenance laws and regulations, repatriation and cultural protection ethics and regulations, best practices in digital asset management, project management systems and software Skills: Adobe creative suite, organizational skills and attention to detail, ability to work collaboratively with coworkers throughout the museum Compensation In addition to a competitive benefits package, annualized pay for this role is typically between $52,845.22 - $71,496.48 covering the middle half of market compensation. Actual compensation will vary based on individual qualifications and work schedule if less than full-time. Position Type Regular Please attach BOTH a current resume and a cover letter in order for your application to be considered for this position . Be sure you have provided all attachments before submitting your application. You will NOT be able to attach additional files after you have hit the Submit button. Review of applications will begin August 3, 2026 About Smith College Located in Northampton, MA, Smith College is one of the largest women’s colleges in the country and is dedicated to excellence in teaching and research across the liberal arts.  A faculty of outstanding scholars interact with students in small classes, as advisors, and through student-faculty research projects. The College is a member of the Five College Consortium with Amherst, Hampshire and Mt. Holyoke Colleges, and the University of Massachusetts Amherst.  Students cross-enroll and faculty cross-teach across the Five Colleges. Consistent with the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) and Massachusetts General Law, Chapter 151B, it is the policy of Smith College to provide reasonable accommodation when requested by a qualified applicant or employee with a disability, unless such accommodation would cause an undue hardship. The policy regarding requests for reasonable accommodation applies to all aspects of employment, including the application process. If reasonable accommodation is needed, please contact our recruiting team at recruiting-u@smith.edu . As set forth in our mission and values , Smith College is committed to promoting a culture of  equity and inclusion among students, staff and faculty.  The College will not discriminate in employment on the basis of age, race, color, ethnicity, national origin, creed, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, pregnancy, genetic information, age, veteran status, physical or mental disability, or any other classification protected by law. Smith College is an equal opportunity employer and complies with all state and federal laws that prohibit discrimination."
    },
    "https://smithcollege.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/smithcollege/job/Smith-College/Drupal-and-Integrations-Developer_R-202600314": {
      "title": "Drupal and Integrations Developer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-06-26\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nIf you have any questions about the position or our application process, reach out to us at recruiting-u@smith.edu . Department Communications and Marketing Job Description Job Summary Maintain, manage and improve mission critical and/or complex enterprise applications and associated services and workflows. Support service requests and collaborate with stakeholders to deploy solutions using assigned application(s). Oversee troubleshooting from routine to highly complex issues and escalates to vendors.  Maintain security within the application(s) and manage routine updates and patching, as well as coordinating more complex system upgrades.  Contribute to the development of documentation and standards. Lead large size projects associated with service delivery or maintenance. Work closely with key stakeholders and supports continuous improvement, as well as providing input for strategic planning. May be called upon after hours and/or weekends to address urgent issues. Take appropriate actions to support a diverse workforce and participate in the College's efforts to create a respectful, inclusive, and welcoming work environment. Essential Functions Technical Engineering & Core Development (40%) : Develop and maintain custom Drupal 10+ modules using modern APIs and best practices. Design and implement secure API integrations with enterprise platforms (Salesforce, Slate, Workday). Maintain and modernize legacy PHP codebases to ensure long-term sustainability and compatibility with current architecture. Strategic Marketing Partnership (25%): Partner with the Marketing Director to leverage analytics tracking, GA4 events, and server-side tagging. Architect 'author-friendly' administrative interfaces (Paragraphs) to empower non-technical content managers. Monitor and optimize Core Web Vitals and site speed to support SEO and brand performance. Systems Maintenance & DevOps (25%): Manage hosting environments (e.g., Pantheon), including deployment pipelines (CI/CD), configuration management, regression testing, security and version updates. Act as primary technical troubleshooter for high-traffic institutional events. Documentation & Collaboration (10%): Document technical implementations and integration patterns to ensure team redundancy. Consult with the Marketing Director and Web Lead during planning phases to provide feasibility assessments for new digital initiatives.   Perform other duties as assigned by management. Minimum Requirements: Bachelor's Degree, Field: Computer Science or Engineering 5 or more years experience. Area: Drupal backend and integrations development Knowledge of: Drupal 10+ architecture, REST APIs, JSON, OAuth, and authentication workflows. Skills: Proficiency in Drupal, Javascript, PHP, Composer and Git; ability to translate complex technical requirements for marketing stakeholders. Preferred Qualifications: Graduate Degree, Field: Computer Science 8 or more years experience, Area: Drupal, Wordpress and API development Knowledge of: Drupal 10+ architecture, REST APIs, JSON, OAuth, and authentication workflows, WordPress, Salesforce Marketing Cloud, Workday, Technolutions Slate CRM Skills: Proficiency in Drupal and WordPress architecture JavaScript, React, PHP, Composer and Git; extensive experience with modern APIs and ETL processes Compensation In addition to a competitive benefits package, annualized pay for this role is typically between $97,972.09 - $132,550.47 covering the middle half of market compensation. Actual compensation will vary based on individual qualifications and work schedule if less than full-time. Position Type Regular Please attach BOTH a current resume and a cover letter in order for your application to be considered for this position . Be sure you have provided all attachments before submitting your application. You will NOT be able to attach additional files after you have hit the Submit button. Review of applications will begin July 13, 2026 About Smith College Located in Northampton, MA, Smith College is one of the largest women’s colleges in the country and is dedicated to excellence in teaching and research across the liberal arts.  A faculty of outstanding scholars interact with students in small classes, as advisors, and through student-faculty research projects. The College is a member of the Five College Consortium with Amherst, Hampshire and Mt. Holyoke Colleges, and the University of Massachusetts Amherst.  Students cross-enroll and faculty cross-teach across the Five Colleges. Consistent with the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) and Massachusetts General Law, Chapter 151B, it is the policy of Smith College to provide reasonable accommodation when requested by a qualified applicant or employee with a disability, unless such accommodation would cause an undue hardship. The policy regarding requests for reasonable accommodation applies to all aspects of employment, including the application process. If reasonable accommodation is needed, please contact our recruiting team at recruiting-u@smith.edu . As set forth in our mission and values , Smith College is committed to promoting a culture of  equity and inclusion among students, staff and faculty.  The College will not discriminate in employment on the basis of age, race, color, ethnicity, national origin, creed, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, pregnancy, genetic information, age, veteran status, physical or mental disability, or any other classification protected by law. Smith College is an equal opportunity employer and complies with all state and federal laws that prohibit discrimination."
    },
    "https://smithcollege.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/smithcollege/job/Smith-College/Substitute-Teacher_R-202200451": {
      "title": "Substitute Teacher",
      "description": "Start date: 2025-10-29\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nIf you have any questions about the position or our application process, reach out to us at recruiting-u@smith.edu . Job Description Campus School of Smith College Elementary School K- 6 PRIMARY FUNCTION(S): In the absence of the regular classroom teacher, provides meaningful instruction in order to help students learn subject matter and/or skills for scheduled lessons. Maintains a class environment favorable to learning and personal growth.  Establishes effective rapport with students and manages classroom effectively. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES:   Classroom: Implements the instructional plans left by the classroom teacher.  Creates a classroom environment that is conducive to learning.  Guides the learning process toward the achievement of curriculum goals and objectives.  Maintains a neat and orderly classroom.  Records notes/observations for classroom teacher.  MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS :  Education/Experience:   Bachelor’s Degree required.  Teacher licensure preferred. Compensation: The daily rate for   substitute teacher is $130.   Position Type Fixed Term Please attach BOTH a current resume and a cover letter in order for your application to be considered for this position. Be sure you have provided all attachments before submitting your application. You will NOT be able to attach additional files after you have hit the Submit button. About Smith College Located in Northampton, MA, Smith College is one of the largest women’s colleges in the country and is dedicated to excellence in teaching and research across the liberal arts.  A faculty of outstanding scholars interact with students in small classes, as advisors, and through student-faculty research projects. The College is a member of the Five College Consortium with Amherst, Hampshire and Mt. Holyoke Colleges, and the University of Massachusetts Amherst. Students cross-enroll and faculty cross-teach across the Five Colleges. Consistent with the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) and Massachusetts General Law, Chapter 151B, it is the policy of Smith College to provide reasonable accommodation when requested by a qualified applicant or employee with a disability, unless such accommodation would cause an undue hardship. The policy regarding requests for reasonable accommodation applies to all aspects of employment, including the application process. If reasonable accommodation is needed, please contact our recruiting team at recruiting-u@smith.edu . As set forth in our mission and values , Smith College is committed to promoting a culture of  equity and inclusion among students, staff and faculty.  The College will not discriminate in employment on the basis of age, race, color, ethnicity, national origin, creed, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, pregnancy, genetic information, age, veteran status, physical or mental disability, or any other classification protected by law. Smith College is an equal opportunity employer and complies with all state and federal laws that prohibit discrimination."
    },
    "https://smithcollege.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/smithcollege/job/Smith-College/Research-Computing-Specialist_R-202600638": {
      "title": "Research Computing Specialist",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-09\nPosted: Posted 5 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nIf you have any questions about the position or our application process, reach out to us at recruiting-u@smith.edu . Department Clark Science Center Administration Job Description Job Summary Reporting to the Director of the Computing and Technical Services (CATS) group, the Research Computing Specialist provides technical support for research, teaching, and administrative computing across the Science Center. This position supports faculty, students, and staff using macOS, Windows, and Linux systems in research labs, classrooms, and offices. The position supports scientific computing workflows, research software environments, high-performance computing (HPC) resources, research data storage, and emerging technologies including artificial intelligence and machine learning (AI/ML). Responsibilities include deployment and lifecycle management of research and laboratory desktop and laptop systems, support for specialized scientific software and instrumentation, participation in server and storage support, and assistance to faculty and students with research computing needs. The role also provides training and documentation related to scientific software, computational methods, data-intensive workflows, and evolving research technologies. Working collaboratively with other CATS staff, the position provides opportunities to develop additional expertise in research computing technologies including HPC, research data storage, Linux systems, and AI/ML computing. Take appropriate actions to support a diverse workforce and participate in the College's efforts to create a respectful, inclusive, and welcoming work environment. Essential Functions Research and Academic Computing Support (50%): As part of the CATS team, assist in the deployment, maintenance, and support of Science Center classroom, laboratory, research, and faculty computing environments across macOS, Windows, and Linux platforms. Provide support for centralized software deployment, operating system management, endpoint security, patching, and lifecycle management for Science Center computing devices.   Manage and support research and laboratory workstations, printers, and mobile devices including macOS, Windows, Linux, iOS, and related platforms. Support faculty and student research computing workflows, including installation and troubleshooting of scientific software, programming environments, analytical applications, AI/ML tools, and software associated with laboratory instrumentation and data acquisition systems.   Assist faculty, students, and research groups with software and hardware selection, storage requirements, data workflows, and research computing technologies. Maintain hardware and software inventory records and technical documentation, and assist with lifecycle replacement planning. Participate in the development and implementation of standards and practices that support research and instructional computing within the Science Center. Research Computing Infrastructure and Systems Administration (25%): Serve as part of the CATS team supporting Linux and Windows servers and infrastructure critical to Science Center operations and research. Under the guidance of senior CATS staff, assist with the operation and support of research computing infrastructure including HPC resources, research data storage systems, AI/ML computing resources, backups, and related services. Support scientific computing services including shared storage, databases, remote access systems, web applications, research collaboration tools, and related infrastructure. Assist with monitoring, troubleshooting, maintenance, documentation, and security of server, storage, and network infrastructure.   Work with CATS staff and central ITS to support system reliability, patching, backups, security, and disaster recovery. Participate in the testing and implementation of new research computing technologies and infrastructure upgrades. User Training, Documentation, and Consulting (20%): Working with other CATS staff, develop and provide technical training, tutorials, workshops, and documentation related to research computing, scientific software, AI/ML tools, and academic technology. Assist faculty, staff, and students in adopting technologies and computational methods that support teaching and research.   Create and maintain technical documentation and knowledge base materials for supported systems and services. Continue professional development through coursework, conferences, workshops, and independent study to develop expertise and remain current with evolving research computing technologies and best practices. Web and Digital Services Support (5%): Assist with maintenance of Science Center web services and online tools as needed, including technical support for departmental websites, online forms, Google Groups management, and related infrastructure. Provide limited support for Science Center digital signage and related communication systems. MINIMUM REQUIRED Qualifications (education, experience, certifications, licenses, knowledge, skills) Bachelor's Degree in a scientific discipline or related field, or an equivalent combination of education and experience Minimum of 5 years experience with a focus on supporting cross-platform client systems and computing technologies in a higher education, research, or enterprise environment, including experience supporting macOS and Windows systems. Familiarity with Linux systems preferred. Demonstrated knowledge of desktop, endpoint, and network computing, especially macOS and Windows systems administration and support in a managed environment. Familiarity with Linux support and administration is desirable. Familiarity with centralized workstation management systems, endpoint security tools, software deployment platforms, scripting, automation, and command-line tools. Strong troubleshooting, organizational, and problem-solving skills with the ability to quickly learn and support new technologies. Ability to communicate technical concepts effectively to non-technical audiences and provide high-quality customer support in a collaborative academic environment. Excellent written, verbal, presentation, interpersonal, and teamwork skills required. Preferred Qualifications Experience or familiarity with scientific or research computing environments, HPC resources, research data storage systems, AI/ML computing, or centralized endpoint management systems preferred. Familiarity with scripting, automation, software packaging, and command-line administration desirable. Experience supporting scientific instrumentation, analytical software, or data-intensive research environments is a plus. Familiarity with web platforms and collaboration tools, including WordPress and Google Workspace administration, is desirable.     Compensation In addition to a competitive benefits package, annualized pay for this role is typically between $76,535.64 - $103,548.21 covering the middle half of market compensation. Actual compensation will vary based on individual qualifications and work schedule if less than full-time. Position Type Regular Please attach BOTH a current resume and a cover letter in order for your application to be considered for this position . Be sure you have provided all attachments before submitting your application. You will NOT be able to attach additional files after you have hit the Submit button. Review of applications will begin November 2, 2026 About Smith College Located in Northampton, MA, Smith College is one of the largest women’s colleges in the country and is dedicated to excellence in teaching and research across the liberal arts.  A faculty of outstanding scholars interact with students in small classes, as advisors, and through student-faculty research projects. The College is a member of the Five College Consortium with Amherst, Hampshire and Mt. Holyoke Colleges, and the University of Massachusetts Amherst.  Students cross-enroll and faculty cross-teach across the Five Colleges. Consistent with the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) and Massachusetts General Law, Chapter 151B, it is the policy of Smith College to provide reasonable accommodation when requested by a qualified applicant or employee with a disability, unless such accommodation would cause an undue hardship. The policy regarding requests for reasonable accommodation applies to all aspects of employment, including the application process. If reasonable accommodation is needed, please contact our recruiting team at recruiting-u@smith.edu . As set forth in our mission and values , Smith College is committed to promoting a culture of  equity and inclusion among students, staff and faculty.  The College will not discriminate in employment on the basis of age, race, color, ethnicity, national origin, creed, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, pregnancy, genetic information, age, veteran status, physical or mental disability, or any other classification protected by law. Smith College is an equal opportunity employer and complies with all state and federal laws that prohibit discrimination."
    },
    "https://smithcollege.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/smithcollege/job/Smith-College/Laborer_R-202600637": {
      "title": "Laborer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-07\nPosted: Posted 7 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nIf you have any questions about the position or our application process, reach out to us at recruiting-u@smith.edu . Department Facilities Management Job Description PRIMARY FUNCTION(S) Under the direction of the manager of grounds services or designee, safely maintain, repair and improve campus grounds, roads, walkways and parking lots. DUTIES Must be able to perform general laborers tasks including, but not limited to, mowing lawns, trimming hedges, loading trucks, removing debris, snow removal as well as drive vehicles. Must be able to safely operate college equipment and vehicles in the maintenance of campus grounds. Must be capable of safely operating snow plow equipment and hand operated snow blower equipment during the winter months as assigned. Observe OSHA safety regulations and practices at all times and participate in required safety programs. Complete assigned project work. Safely drive college equipment and vehicles as assigned. Responsible for cleaning equipment/vehicles, inspecting for damage and performing visual check prior to using. Reporting any issues to the supervisor immediately Maintain positive public relations with all members of the college community. Provide back-up staffing as necessary. Perform other related duties as assigned. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: Must have a valid driver’s license. Must complete and comply with all college driving credentialing requirements and obtain DOT certificate, if requested. Upon award of this position, a Division of Motor Vehicles background check will be completed. Must be able to safely lift and carry up to fifty pounds regularly and up to ninety pounds occasionally.  Must be able to bend, walk, stand, climb stairs and ladders and safely operate loaded hand trucks and dollies. Driver shall not have a history of moving vehicle violations or vehicle traffic accidents. Must demonstrate proficiency in accessing the internet, using the Smith College email, finance, payroll and calendar systems as well as the work order system. Must be able to pass a background check. Education/Experience: High school/vocational diploma, GED equivalent or combination of education and experience. Minimum of 1 year (preferred) grounds maintenance experience. Skills:  Ability to use good judgment to take appropriate, independent action to carry out the primary functions of the job. Must possess the ability to carry out oral and written (electronic) instructions. Must be able to safely operate departmental vehicles and equipment including, but not limited to, riding mowers, lawn mowers, hand mowers, snow blowers, snow removal equipment, chainsaws, clippers, and hedge trimmers, demolishing walls, hand digging, hand tools and raking leaves with hand rakes and blowers. Must be able to safely lift and carry various types of furnishings, equipment and materials.   Must be able to kneel, crouch and bend repetitively for extended periods. Must possess strong commitment to maintain high standards of maintenance. Must have knowledge of OSHA regulations for the safety of the college employees, its users and properties. Must demonstrate ability to read and understand Safety Data Sheets (SDS). Must be able to work independently and in a team environment. Must have a clear understanding of the union contract and all college policies. Compensation This position is covered by a collective bargaining agreement with a progressive wage schedule. The Step 1 hourly rate is $23.55. New hires are typically placed at Step 1. Position Type Regular Please attach BOTH a current resume and a cover letter in order for your application to be considered for this position . Be sure you have provided all attachments before submitting your application. You will NOT be able to attach additional files after you have hit the Submit button. Review of applications will begin October 21, 2026 About Smith College Located in Northampton, MA, Smith College is one of the largest women’s colleges in the country and is dedicated to excellence in teaching and research across the liberal arts.  A faculty of outstanding scholars interact with students in small classes, as advisors, and through student-faculty research projects. The College is a member of the Five College Consortium with Amherst, Hampshire and Mt. Holyoke Colleges, and the University of Massachusetts Amherst.  Students cross-enroll and faculty cross-teach across the Five Colleges. Consistent with the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) and Massachusetts General Law, Chapter 151B, it is the policy of Smith College to provide reasonable accommodation when requested by a qualified applicant or employee with a disability, unless such accommodation would cause an undue hardship. The policy regarding requests for reasonable accommodation applies to all aspects of employment, including the application process. If reasonable accommodation is needed, please contact our recruiting team at recruiting-u@smith.edu . As set forth in our mission and values , Smith College is committed to promoting a culture of  equity and inclusion among students, staff and faculty.  The College will not discriminate in employment on the basis of age, race, color, ethnicity, national origin, creed, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, pregnancy, genetic information, age, veteran status, physical or mental disability, or any other classification protected by law. Smith College is an equal opportunity employer and complies with all state and federal laws that prohibit discrimination."
    },
    "https://smithcollege.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/smithcollege/job/Smith-College/Jewish-Student-Advisor_R-202600628": {
      "title": "Jewish Student Advisor",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-01\nPosted: Posted 13 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nIf you have any questions about the position or our application process, reach out to us at recruiting-u@smith.edu . Department Religious and Spiritual Life Job Description Job Summary Oversee Jewish Life on Campus, provide advising and guidance in an extraordinarily diverse campus environment that is multifaith, pluralistic, and secular, in which community members access Jewishness through both religious and secular means. Be a resource for all Jewish students of varied backgrounds, affiliations, political, and cultural identities. Preside over Jewish religious services. Collaborate with director and CRSL team to promote initiatives in the areas of interfaith collaboration, spiritual flourishing, religious literacy, contemplative practice, ethical reflection. Implement a wide range of activities and educational opportunities for student religious practice, spiritual development, cultural identity formation, and other areas which promote well-being. Help students build essential capacities in alignment with Smith’s strategic plan, such as self-awareness and the ability to engage across difference. Take appropriate actions to support a diverse workforce and participate in the College's efforts to create a respectful, inclusive, and welcoming work environment. Essential Functions Oversee Jewish Life (65%): Provide spiritual counsel and religious guidance for all Jewish students of varying levels and types of observations of Judaism. Organize and lead weekly Shabbat prayers and major Jewish holidays (such as Rosh Hashanah, Yom Kippur, Pesach, Sukkot, and Simchat Torah), and give dvar torah reflections throughout the year as needed. Provide religious leadership and pulpit leadership on major holidays (provide or seek out and oversee pulpit support as needed). Plan and execute meaningful religious services for community. Help students navigate the challenges and opportunities of being a religious and cultural minority. Facilitate dialogue between groups when there may be tension. Provide education to the wider community on legacies and contemporary realities of antisemitism, making and suggesting best intervention strategies. Consult with CRSL staff about issues of importance to the Jewish community. Provide pastoral care to non-Jewish as well as Jewish students and help facilitate other CRSL Programs related to CRSL mission such as center-wide efforts in support of human rights in higher education and beyond. Collaborate with local community on programs related to Jewish life. Model savviness in engaging across differences of perspective and identity with a wide range of constituents, including not only students but also administration, campus leadership, alums, prospective students and their communities/families. Collaborate with CRSL staff on broader CRSL efforts and programs. Provide outreach and spiritual care to Jewish students. Provide education and engagement opportunities to a wide range of religious orientations. Liase with faculty from the Religious and Jewish Studies Department as well as other departments when there are intersecting goals. Oversee Kosher Kitchen and help supervise cooks, consult with dining about kosher needs. Oversee other aspects of adherence to Jewish law and ritual. Liaise with students who elect not to participate in Jewish life at Smith to ensure that their religious needs are met within the broader community. Eagerness to partner with local synagogues. Eagerness to partner with Jewish student professionals at MHC, Amherst, and at UMass-Hillel and to engage Smith students with regional, national, and international Jewish student networks, organizations, and opportunities. Supervise Smith Jewish Organization Advise “SCJC” the student Jewish organization, and other Smith organizations as appropriate; including helping with planning logistics, providing guidance in leadership formation, and helping oversee group process. Liase between CRSL leadership and organization about programming, collaborations and interfaith engagement. Attend weekly leadership meetings to provide guidance as needed, consult with group leadership. Organize all weekly Shabbat services, leading at least two services per month. Organize and support festivals. Provide infrastructure for and participate in the organization of High Holiday services, the building of a campus sukkah and required ritual items, nightly Hanukkah candlelighting and party, Smith Passover seder. Plan and preside over Jewish holidays as well as weekly Shabbat services Plan and execute meaningful religious services for community. Provide spoken and sung offerings, offer inspiring Dvar Torah reflections. Collaborate with students and other stakeholders to determine service content. Provide spiritual care to Jewish and non-Jewish students Provide a welcoming and compassionate presence to students in individual and group settings. Offer spiritual guidance through a lens of equity and justice. Maintain regular appointments with students who request them and hold office hours for periodic consultations. Liase with other campus care providers as needed/ is appropriate. Help students explore Jewish identity, help potential converts through discernment process. Other Functions ( 35%): Collaborate with team members on all aspects of the CRSL mission and participate in the planning and implementation of college-wide programming related to Diversity, Equity and inclusion. Other duties as assigned. All employees are expected to participate in the College's efforts to create a respectful, inclusive, and welcoming work environment. Minimum Qualifications Required: Master's degree required (or in process with equivalent experience). Broad understanding of Judaism, Jewish history, and Jewish liturgical texts required, including ability to lead services and guide others in singing and prayer. Familiarity with the particulars of kashrut necessary to be able to supervise kosher dining. 3-5 years of direct work experience in the Jewish world or affiliated responsibilities, preferably in a higher education setting and/or with emerging adults. Crisis intervention and dialogic skills. Experience working with individuals from diverse backgrounds. Preferred Qualifications: Rabbinic and/or Cantorial Ordination or PhD in Jewish Studies or a related discipline preferred but not required. Skills: Ability to deal with sensitive personal and interpersonal subjects and material. Demonstrated ability to communicate clearly and concisely, both orally and in writing; exercise effective independent professional judgment; ability to establish and maintain collaborative working relationships with students, student organizations, faculty, staff, and external vendors; and relate successfully in one-on-one and group situations with persons of diverse backgrounds and interests. Skill with electronic outreach and marketing, ability to plan and promote programs. Excellent listening skills. Able to identify growing edges and set goals, open to feedback. Ability to initiate and follow tasks through completion. Compensation In addition to a competitive benefits package, annualized pay for this role is typically between $64,386.77 - $87,111.51 covering the middle half of market compensation. Actual compensation will vary based on individual qualifications and work schedule if less than full-time. Position Type Regular Please attach BOTH a current resume and a cover letter in order for your application to be considered for this position . Be sure you have provided all attachments before submitting your application. You will NOT be able to attach additional files after you have hit the Submit button. Review of applications will begin October 21, 2026 About Smith College Located in Northampton, MA, Smith College is one of the largest women’s colleges in the country and is dedicated to excellence in teaching and research across the liberal arts.  A faculty of outstanding scholars interact with students in small classes, as advisors, and through student-faculty research projects. The College is a member of the Five College Consortium with Amherst, Hampshire and Mt. Holyoke Colleges, and the University of Massachusetts Amherst.  Students cross-enroll and faculty cross-teach across the Five Colleges. Consistent with the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) and Massachusetts General Law, Chapter 151B, it is the policy of Smith College to provide reasonable accommodation when requested by a qualified applicant or employee with a disability, unless such accommodation would cause an undue hardship. The policy regarding requests for reasonable accommodation applies to all aspects of employment, including the application process. If reasonable accommodation is needed, please contact our recruiting team at recruiting-u@smith.edu . As set forth in our mission and values , Smith College is committed to promoting a culture of  equity and inclusion among students, staff and faculty.  The College will not discriminate in employment on the basis of age, race, color, ethnicity, national origin, creed, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, pregnancy, genetic information, age, veteran status, physical or mental disability, or any other classification protected by law. Smith College is an equal opportunity employer and complies with all state and federal laws that prohibit discrimination."
    },
    "https://smithcollege.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/smithcollege/job/Smith-College/Substitute-Nurse--Campus-School_R-202600558": {
      "title": "Substitute Nurse, Campus School",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-01\nPosted: Posted 13 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nIf you have any questions about the position or our application process, reach out to us at recruiting-u@smith.edu . Job Description Campus School of Smith College Elementary School K- 6 PRIMARY FUNCTION(S): In the absence of the school nurse the substitute nurse will follow the Campus School health office policy and protocols.including: Respond to and record student illness, medical needs and injuries as they occur during the school day. Administer restricted medication to students Communicate with parents regarding illnesses, or injury to students. Minimum Qualifications: RN licensure, two year nursing experience that includes working with elementary age children. Compensation Pay for this role is typically between $35-$37 hourly. Actual compensation will vary based on individual qualifications. Position Type Fixed Term Please attach BOTH a current resume and a cover letter in order for your application to be considered for this position. Be sure you have provided all attachments before submitting your application. You will NOT be able to attach additional files after you have hit the Submit button. About Smith College Located in Northampton, MA, Smith College is one of the largest women’s colleges in the country and is dedicated to excellence in teaching and research across the liberal arts.  A faculty of outstanding scholars interact with students in small classes, as advisors, and through student-faculty research projects. The College is a member of the Five College Consortium with Amherst, Hampshire and Mt. Holyoke Colleges, and the University of Massachusetts Amherst. Students cross-enroll and faculty cross-teach across the Five Colleges. Consistent with the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) and Massachusetts General Law, Chapter 151B, it is the policy of Smith College to provide reasonable accommodation when requested by a qualified applicant or employee with a disability, unless such accommodation would cause an undue hardship. The policy regarding requests for reasonable accommodation applies to all aspects of employment, including the application process. If reasonable accommodation is needed, please contact our recruiting team at recruiting-u@smith.edu . As set forth in our mission and values , Smith College is committed to promoting a culture of  equity and inclusion among students, staff and faculty.  The College will not discriminate in employment on the basis of age, race, color, ethnicity, national origin, creed, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, pregnancy, genetic information, age, veteran status, physical or mental disability, or any other classification protected by law. Smith College is an equal opportunity employer and complies with all state and federal laws that prohibit discrimination."
