    "https://smithcollege.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/smithcollege/job/Smith-College/Administrative-Coordinator_R-202600614": {
      "title": "Administrative Coordinator",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-30\nPosted: Posted 14 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nIf you have any questions about the position or our application process, reach out to us at recruiting-u@smith.edu . Department Dance Job Description Smith College is hiring an Administrative Coordinator to power the operations of our active Dance Department. Balancing a rigorous academic curriculum with an ambitious performance schedule, this role requires exceptional administrative, budget, and planning skills. If you have a passion for the performing arts and a proven track record in administrative management and collaboration, we welcome your application. Job Summary As a member of an administrative team, under supervision of the administrative manager, provide comprehensive administrative support in all areas of operation by working collaboratively with program faculty and staff to support the curricular/pedagogical mission of the assigned academic units.  Assist in the general support of the daily operations in an academic building. Take appropriate actions to support a diverse workforce and participate in the College's efforts to create a respectful, inclusive, and welcoming work environment. Essential Functions Administrative Support (25%) Handle clerical, ordering, and scheduling tasks for the department chair, graduate program director, program faculty, and staff.  Maintain departmental records, calendars, and databases. Manage departmental correspondence draft publicity and other communications for distribution to faculty, students, alumnae and other external audiences. Prepare materials for departmental meetings and disseminate actions points and decisions. Support departmental faculty and staff searches, decennial reviews, midterm reviews, and the annual audition to the Master in Fine Arts program. Maintain and update departmental web page content and other online presence, as appropriate. Assist in onboarding of new faculty and staff.  Act as administrative liaison with other departments, offices and institutions, such as Five College Dance, the Department of Theatre, and Smith Office of the Arts (SOFA). Budget Support: (20%) Administer and track all program budgets based on institutional policy and using the college’s financial management systems. Perform budget operations, process requisitions, purchase order requests, invoices, and reimbursement requests. Process the contracting of vendors and services, and the purchase of advertisement.  Analyze expenditures against the budget and notify directors of discrepancies. Advise, support and proactively work with department chairs on departmental budget planning. Reconcile and report on key budget areas. Event management: (20%) Schedule, support, organize, and publicize all aspects of departmental events, visits, residencies, rehearsals, field trips, and auditions. Support the logistics of dance concerts in coordination with the production team. Partner with other offices and units, including Five College Dance, SOFA and Media Relations, for publicity of events. Manage events and lecture series including budget, funding, travel and honorarium.  Plan annual commencement events. Organize the work of appointed student liaisons in connection with event planning.                   Curricular Support (20%) Assemble and update course offerings and schedules. Assist in the preparation of annual course catalog revisions.  Support course scheduling process and work with departments to meet institutional deadlines around curricular requests and changes. Act as a liaison with Registrar and Provost offices on curricular matters. Order instructional supplies and help prepare course materials. Secure copyright permissions, following college policy. Assist faculty in hiring work-study students.  Monitor and update yearly departmental records. Other Functions Facilities Oversight: (15%) General oversight of the department' s office and dance studios including coordinating work orders, keys, office equipment, and supplies. Weekly inspection of all spaces. Manage the reservations and use of the dance studios and conference room. Maintain building equipment; report and track building repairs. Manage building budgets with decision making on expenditures and reconciliation. Order supplies and manage inventory. Coordinate the use of spaces shared with other departments. Act as a liaison with faculty and allied offices to facilitate faculty onboarding and office moves. Supervise Student Workers: Hire, train, and supervise student workers to support the above-listed duties and responsibilities. MINIMUM REQUIRED Qualifications (education, experience, certifications, licenses, knowledge, skills) High school degree or equivalent and three years of relevant experience or the equivalent combination of education and/or experience. Experience working with individuals from diverse backgrounds. Skills Excellent oral and written communication skills; very strong skills in MacOS and apps in Google Workspace (Google Docs, Sheets, Calendar, Forms, etc); proficiency in Microsoft Word, Microsoft Excel; skills in designing basic fliers/posters and managing social media accounts; ability to organize and prioritize work effectively, be detail oriented, take initiative and responsibility, be pro-active, resolve problems, manage multiple tasks simultaneously, and work well with diverse groups of faculty and staff. Willingness to learn new technologies as necessary. Compensation In addition to a competitive benefits package, pay for this role is typically between $25.23 - $30.84 hourly covering the middle half of market compensation. Actual compensation will vary based on individual qualifications. Position Type Regular Please attach BOTH a current resume and a cover letter in order for your application to be considered for this position . Be sure you have provided all attachments before submitting your application. You will NOT be able to attach additional files after you have hit the Submit button. Review of applications will begin October 8, 2026 About Smith College Located in Northampton, MA, Smith College is one of the largest women’s colleges in the country and is dedicated to excellence in teaching and research across the liberal arts.  A faculty of outstanding scholars interact with students in small classes, as advisors, and through student-faculty research projects. The College is a member of the Five College Consortium with Amherst, Hampshire and Mt. Holyoke Colleges, and the University of Massachusetts Amherst.  Students cross-enroll and faculty cross-teach across the Five Colleges. Consistent with the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) and Massachusetts General Law, Chapter 151B, it is the policy of Smith College to provide reasonable accommodation when requested by a qualified applicant or employee with a disability, unless such accommodation would cause an undue hardship. The policy regarding requests for reasonable accommodation applies to all aspects of employment, including the application process. If reasonable accommodation is needed, please contact our recruiting team at recruiting-u@smith.edu . As set forth in our mission and values , Smith College is committed to promoting a culture of  equity and inclusion among students, staff and faculty.  The College will not discriminate in employment on the basis of age, race, color, ethnicity, national origin, creed, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, pregnancy, genetic information, age, veteran status, physical or mental disability, or any other classification protected by law. Smith College is an equal opportunity employer and complies with all state and federal laws that prohibit discrimination."
    },
    "https://smithcollege.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/smithcollege/job/Smith-College/Campus-School-Director-of-Development---Alumni-Relations_R-202600617-1": {
      "title": "Campus School Director of Development & Alumni Relations",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-29\nPosted: Posted 15 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nIf you have any questions about the position or our application process, reach out to us at recruiting-u@smith.edu . Department Campus School Job Description Job Summary The Director of Development and Alumni Relations reports to the Head of School and works closely with the college’s development office to plan and oversee all Campus School fundraising operations, including the annual fund, Family School Partnership fundraising, planned giving, and alumni relations. This position is responsible for the entirety of the advancement operations of Campus School. This position involves continuing to build an annual fund program to attract and maintain gifts to the Campus School from current and past families, alumni, staff members, and friends of the school. Take appropriate actions to support a diverse workforce and participate in the College's efforts to create a respectful, inclusive, and welcoming work environment. Essential Functions Development (45%) Responsible for planning short- and long-term advancement strategy as well as designing appeals, communicating effectively with our wide range of constituents, and creating opportunities for giving and actively soliciting gifts from individuals and/or foundations to the annual fund, to support capital projects, to help fund special appeals, and through planned giving are key components of this role.   Oversee processing of all gifts, including receipts, and maintaining all giving records. Thanking and stewarding donors with personalized follow up notes, email messages, and in the case of higher potential donors, visits is critical to the success of this position.   An additional responsibility will be to occasionally write grant proposals, often in collaboration with the college, to generate funds for special projects and events.   Alumni Relations (45%) As we celebrate our Centennial in 2026-27, continue to develop and execute a strategic approach to Alumni Relations, continuing to build our alumni database with contact information and then communicating to this important constituency and reconnecting them with the school. Research, identify, and solicit key donor prospects in coordination with the Head of School and the Smith College Alumni Relations and Development Office. Increasing and empowering the Centennial Committee to shape and execute the celebration of our 100th anniversary and raise more money, awareness and maintain connections in support of the school.   Work closely with the Family School Partnership, serving as their primary liaison with the school. In addition to setting agendas and meeting monthly with the leadership of the Family School Partnership to strategize and plan, help implement traditional school community events such as the Welcome Reception, the Fall Fair, Movie/Game Night, the Spring Soirée, Field Day, and the End-of-School Picnic   Other administrative work (10%) Act as a key participant on the Campus School administrative team including attending administration meetings and staff meetings; serving on committees; participating in school events and programs. Perform other duties as assigned by the Head of School MINIMUM REQUIRED Qualifications (education, experience, certifications, licenses, knowledge, skills) Bachelor’s Degree and 8+ years of experience with a demonstrated track record of outstanding leadership, interpersonal, organizational, analytical, and oral/written communication skills; ability to self-direct work and collaborate with colleagues while effectively planning and executing a challenging array of program initiatives; ability to manage confidential information with appropriate discretion and tact; and strong computer skills and technical literacy. Preferred Qualifications Graduate Degree and 12+ years of experience with fundraising and alumni relations in independent school setting. Demonstrated success in fundraising and alumni cultivation in an independent school or college. Knowledge of trends in fundraising and familiarity with development software and databases. Compensation In addition to a competitive benefits package, annualized pay for this role is typically between $80,410.25 - $108,790.34 covering the middle half of market compensation. Actual compensation will vary based on individual qualifications and work schedule if less than full-time. Position Type Fixed Term Please attach BOTH a current resume and a cover letter in order for your application to be considered for this position . Be sure you have provided all attachments before submitting your application. You will NOT be able to attach additional files after you have hit the Submit button. Review of applications will begin October 12, 2026 About Smith College Located in Northampton, MA, Smith College is one of the largest women’s colleges in the country and is dedicated to excellence in teaching and research across the liberal arts.  A faculty of outstanding scholars interact with students in small classes, as advisors, and through student-faculty research projects. The College is a member of the Five College Consortium with Amherst, Hampshire and Mt. Holyoke Colleges, and the University of Massachusetts Amherst.  Students cross-enroll and faculty cross-teach across the Five Colleges. Consistent with the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) and Massachusetts General Law, Chapter 151B, it is the policy of Smith College to provide reasonable accommodation when requested by a qualified applicant or employee with a disability, unless such accommodation would cause an undue hardship. The policy regarding requests for reasonable accommodation applies to all aspects of employment, including the application process. If reasonable accommodation is needed, please contact our recruiting team at recruiting-u@smith.edu . As set forth in our mission and values , Smith College is committed to promoting a culture of  equity and inclusion among students, staff and faculty.  The College will not discriminate in employment on the basis of age, race, color, ethnicity, national origin, creed, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, pregnancy, genetic information, age, veteran status, physical or mental disability, or any other classification protected by law. Smith College is an equal opportunity employer and complies with all state and federal laws that prohibit discrimination."
    },
    "https://smithcollege.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/smithcollege/job/Smith-College/Sworn-Police-Officer_R-202600618": {
      "title": "Sworn Police Officer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-29\nPosted: Posted 15 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nIf you have any questions about the position or our application process, reach out to us at recruiting-u@smith.edu . Department Campus Safety Job Description Job Summary   As the first point of contact a Smith College police officer is a sworn law enforcement professional responsible for maintaining a safe, secure, bias free education environment. They serve students, staff, faculty and community members and the protection of property through proactive patrols, crime prevention, emergency response, investigation of incidents by enforcing both state laws and college regulations while focusing and striving on community engagement activities. We strive to develop a close relationship with the campus community. Foster relationships by employing different strategies including programs and services that will engage relationship building. The principal responsibilities include patrols of the campus grounds and buildings, while ensuring safety and security of all college community members and property. Take appropriate actions to support a diverse workforce and participate in the College's efforts to create a respectful, inclusive, and welcoming work environment.     Essential Functions Campus Safety Operations (100%): Conduct foot, bike, or vehicular patrols of the college property including buildings, parking lots and grounds to deter crime and ensure safety. Serve as first responders to emergencies, including medical calls, fire, and criminal acts and assist outside departments when they are called onto campus for service. During patrols of campus report any problems that you may come across to on-duty Officers and fill out work orders for Facilities Management if needed. Inspect and report lighting or emergency call box problems on campus. Attend all department mandated trainings and staff meetings as required. Follow department chain of command for notifications and guidance. Maintain confidentiality of department information, records, reports, and logs. Become familiar with department policies and procedures. Maintains an alert, attentive demeanor for the duration of assigned shift. Assist, promote and participate in community engagement activities (as assigned). Employees are expected to participate in the College's efforts to create a respectful, inclusive, and welcoming work & learning environment.   May answer incoming phone lines and provide appropriate assistance to those calls (if dispatch trained). Assist with walk-ins to our office when the situation dictates. Periodically monitor numerous security cameras and access points across campus. Respond to Fire, Trouble alarms and other types of alarms. Accurately log all department calls for service, officer activity, assistance provided into the department’s computer system. Make notifications to College officials and other agencies during emergency and non-emergency situations. Maintain certification on the use of any State or Federal Criminal Justice Information Systems (DCJIS). Support and uphold the department’s mission and community-poling philosophy. Check and secure campus buildings to help prevent crimes from occurring. Open buildings or rooms as assigned. Provide walking escorts and lockouts for members of the College community. Provide non-emergency medical transports via College vehicle (when authorized) to Cooley Dickinson Hospital or other medical appointments. Perform other duties as assigned by management.   MINIMUM REQUIRED Qualifications: High School Diploma or Equivalent 0 to 1 years experience Required Certification(s): Must be POST certified with graduation from a Municipal Police Training Committee (MPTC) academy or Bridge Academy. Required License(s): Must have a valid driver’s license. Must complete and comply with all college driving credentialing requirements. Division of Motor Vehicles background check will be completed prior to a formal hire. Must hold or be able to obtain shortly after hire CPR and First Responder Certification. Must hold and maintain a License to Carry firearms (LTC).   Other: Must be able to pass a criminal and work background check, driving background and a medical pre-screening. Be able to work in a diverse community. Ability to sit, walk and stand for long periods of time. Strong communication, decision-making, and interpersonal skills for a diverse college community is a must.   Skills: Can demonstrate good customer service skills. Ability to work in a fast paced, multi-tasked environment. Ability to function calmly and efficiently in all types of situations including emergencies.   Additional Information: Sworn Police Officers should remember that many times individuals who are interacting with the police department may be upset, irritated and should always be talked to in a calm and rational manner. Treat all persons with dignity and respect.   Compensation In addition to a competitive benefits package, pay for this role is typically between $27.17 - $33.21 hourly covering the middle half of market compensation. Actual compensation will vary based on individual qualifications. Position Type Regular Please attach BOTH a current resume and a cover letter in order for your application to be considered for this position . Be sure you have provided all attachments before submitting your application. You will NOT be able to attach additional files after you have hit the Submit button. Review of applications will begin October 13, 2026 About Smith College Located in Northampton, MA, Smith College is one of the largest women’s colleges in the country and is dedicated to excellence in teaching and research across the liberal arts.  A faculty of outstanding scholars interact with students in small classes, as advisors, and through student-faculty research projects. The College is a member of the Five College Consortium with Amherst, Hampshire and Mt. Holyoke Colleges, and the University of Massachusetts Amherst.  Students cross-enroll and faculty cross-teach across the Five Colleges. Consistent with the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) and Massachusetts General Law, Chapter 151B, it is the policy of Smith College to provide reasonable accommodation when requested by a qualified applicant or employee with a disability, unless such accommodation would cause an undue hardship. The policy regarding requests for reasonable accommodation applies to all aspects of employment, including the application process. If reasonable accommodation is needed, please contact our recruiting team at recruiting-u@smith.edu . As set forth in our mission and values , Smith College is committed to promoting a culture of  equity and inclusion among students, staff and faculty.  The College will not discriminate in employment on the basis of age, race, color, ethnicity, national origin, creed, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, pregnancy, genetic information, age, veteran status, physical or mental disability, or any other classification protected by law. Smith College is an equal opportunity employer and complies with all state and federal laws that prohibit discrimination."
    },
    "https://smithcollege.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/smithcollege/job/Smith-College/Assistant-Director-of-Admission_R-202600616": {
      "title": "Assistant Director of Admission",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-29\nPosted: Posted 15 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nIf you have any questions about the position or our application process, reach out to us at recruiting-u@smith.edu . Department Admission Job Description Job Summary Responsible for the recruitment and evaluation of prospective students. Utilize Slate to create events, outreach communications and support the day-to-day CRM functionality to aid in the overall mission of the Smith College Office of Admission. Take appropriate actions to support a diverse workforce and participate in the College's efforts to create a respectful, inclusive, and welcoming work environment. Essential Functions Recruitment & Yield (30%): Plan and coordinate up to six weeks of travel annually to recruit students and manage an assigned geographic territory determined by the Dean of Admission. Visit high schools and community colleges, attend college fairs, conduct workshops related to applying to college.  Initiate and maintain effective communication between Smith College and college counselors from a diverse range of high schools. Manage communication with students, parents, counselors and other constituencies.  Present on-campus and/or virtual information sessions and lead workshops during events for prospective and admitted students. Other duties, including travel, as assigned.  Admission Reading and Selection (20%): Read, evaluate and participate in admission committee work to decide on candidate admissibility for admission to the College in coordination with a team of colleagues under the direction of the Dean of Admission.   Candidates may include first-year applicants, transfer applicants or students applying through Questbridge or Posse programs. Interview prospective students as needed, including Ada Comstock Scholar applicants.  Slate, Events & Communications (40%): Craft email communications for admission events. Build admission program events, registration forms and surveys using Slate CRM. Serve on the communications and campus visit/admitted student program teams. Assist in the proofing and editing of queries and communications.  Contribute to other communication team projects as assigned.  Under the leadership of the Senior Associate Director of Admission and in concert with the Admission Systems Administrator and the Enrollment Analyst, serve as a contributing member of the Slate team. In alignment with admission office priorities, provide comprehensive Slate support for Smith's Women of Distinction program. Professional Development (10%): Keep abreast of pertinent developments at Smith College, in higher education, and in the field of professional admission work.  Develop skills and connections and pursue professional development opportunities to better understand current issues and challenges in the field of admission and in Higher Education. Cooperate with other administrators of the College where necessary, especially Student Financial Services, Communications & Marketing and the Alumnae Group. Performs related duties as required.   MINIMUM REQUIRED Qualifications: Bachelor's Degree 1 to 5 years experience Required License(s): Driver’s license Skills: The ability to effectively and efficiently develop and execute several key projects simultaneously. Must be able to work independently, have strong organizational and planning skills, excellent writing and oral communication skills. Required strong analytical and technological skills including familiarity with MS Office and Google suites, CRM platforms and the ability to learn Slate CRM specifically and Workday systems. Must have excellent interpersonal skills and the ability to work as part of a team, interact with people of all backgrounds and use sound judgement, creativity and vision. Must hold a valid driver’s license and be able and willing to travel by both car and airplane.  High comfort level with public speaking to large and small groups.  Able and willing to work occasional evenings and weekends. Preferred Qualifications: Knowledge of: Previous experience in a college admission office and with public speaking preferred. Individuals should be able to demonstrate fluidity with a variety of software and database management platforms.  Skills: Proficient in language other than English, especially Spanish Compensation In addition to a competitive benefits package, annualized pay for this role is typically between $50,298.84 - $68,051.37 covering the middle half of market compensation. Actual compensation will vary based on individual qualifications and work schedule if less than full-time. Position Type Regular Please attach BOTH a current resume and a cover letter in order for your application to be considered for this position . Be sure you have provided all attachments before submitting your application. You will NOT be able to attach additional files after you have hit the Submit button. Review of applications will begin October 19, 2026 About Smith College Located in Northampton, MA, Smith College is one of the largest women’s colleges in the country and is dedicated to excellence in teaching and research across the liberal arts.  A faculty of outstanding scholars interact with students in small classes, as advisors, and through student-faculty research projects. The College is a member of the Five College Consortium with Amherst, Hampshire and Mt. Holyoke Colleges, and the University of Massachusetts Amherst.  Students cross-enroll and faculty cross-teach across the Five Colleges. Consistent with the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) and Massachusetts General Law, Chapter 151B, it is the policy of Smith College to provide reasonable accommodation when requested by a qualified applicant or employee with a disability, unless such accommodation would cause an undue hardship. The policy regarding requests for reasonable accommodation applies to all aspects of employment, including the application process. If reasonable accommodation is needed, please contact our recruiting team at recruiting-u@smith.edu . As set forth in our mission and values , Smith College is committed to promoting a culture of  equity and inclusion among students, staff and faculty.  The College will not discriminate in employment on the basis of age, race, color, ethnicity, national origin, creed, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, pregnancy, genetic information, age, veteran status, physical or mental disability, or any other classification protected by law. Smith College is an equal opportunity employer and complies with all state and federal laws that prohibit discrimination."
    },
    "https://smithcollege.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/smithcollege/job/Smith-College/Editorial-Director_R-202600609": {
      "title": "Editorial Director",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-28\nPosted: Posted 16 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nIf you have any questions about the position or our application process, reach out to us at recruiting-u@smith.edu . Department Communications and Marketing Job Description Job Summary The Editorial Director is a highly seasoned editorial professional within the Communications and Marketing team who leads the development and production of Smith College’s flagship print and digital publication — the Smith Quarterly , the college’s alum magazine — as well as for major publications produced in partnership with the Offices of Admissions and Advancement. Working independently and exercising significant editorial judgment, the Editorial Director conceives and executes ambitious, audience-centered storytelling that advances pride and engagement, supports fundraising and enrollment priorities, and reflects the full richness of the Smith community. This role requires an experienced writer and editor who moves fluidly between long-form feature journalism and shorter editorial forms, manages complex production timelines and budgets, and serves as a trusted editorial partner to colleagues across divisions. Take appropriate actions to support a diverse workforce and participate in the College's efforts to create a respectful, inclusive, and welcoming work environment. Essential Functions Smith Quarterly — Alum Magazine (60%) Serves as editor of the Smith Quarterly , exercising editorial judgment over all aspects of the magazine’s content, voice, and direction. Develops each issue’s story budget; conceives, assigns, and edits long-form features, reported essays, alum profiles, and shorter editorial content. Writes features and other content as needed. Manages an Editorial Board and partners with design, photography, and production colleagues to shape visually compelling issues. Identifies, contracts, and edits freelance contributors, managing those relationships from assignment through final edit. Oversees all phases of print production, including managing relationships with printers and mailing houses, tracking timelines, and ensuring quality at every stage. Manages the publication’s budget, including forecasting, tracking expenditures, and making independent decisions about resource allocation. Manages editorial calendars and production schedules across concurrent publications, coordinating timelines with writers, designers, photographers, and external vendors. Anticipates production challenges and resolves them independently. Ensures all publications meet quality standards and deadlines.   Stays current on trends in magazine storytelling, alum engagement, and higher education communications to bring fresh editorial thinking to the work. Major Publications (25%) Serves as editor of major publications produced by the division in partnership with the Offices of Admissions and Advancement, including viewbooks, case statements, donor reports, campaign materials, and other high-visibility print and digital content. Proactively identifies and develops story ideas across all publications — cultivating sources among alum, faculty, students, and staff; monitoring developments in higher education, social work, and in the lives of Smith’s constituencies; and maintaining an ongoing story pipeline that reflects the depth and diversity of the Smith community. Exercises judgment on editorial direction, voice, and quality; works closely with Admissions and Advancement colleagues to ensure publications serve strategic enrollment and fundraising goals while reflecting Smith’s institutional voice. Writes and edits content as needed across these projects. Staff Management (15%) Manages the writer/editors within the Division of Communications and Marketing who create content for smith.edu, marketing materials, and strategic publications, providing editorial direction, feedback, and professional development. Evaluates and evolves the editorial team to best serve the division’s strategic priorities. Ensures consistent quality and voice across staff-produced content. MINIMUM REQUIRED Qualifications (education, experience, certifications, licenses, knowledge, skills) Bachelor's Degree in Journalism, English, Communications or a related field. Minimum of 8 years of progressively responsible experience in editing, writing, or publishing, preferably in a magazine, higher education, or institutional communications context. Working knowledge of InDesign or comparable production tools. Demonstrated expertise in both long-form feature writing and substantive editing. Proven ability to manage complex, multi-stakeholder publication production processes independently. Experience managing budgets and contracting freelance contributors. Excellent news judgment and the ability to develop compelling story ideas across a range of formats and audiences. Experience working with individuals from diverse backgrounds. Skills Exceptional writing and editing skills across long and short forms. Strong editorial vision and independent judgment. Ability to manage multiple complex production timelines and budgets simultaneously. Collaborative and consultative work style; skilled at building relationships across divisions. Audience-centered editorial instincts with an understanding of alum engagement and institutional communications. Detail-oriented and deadline-driven. Familiarity with digital publishing and content presentation best practices. Comfortable working independently on ambitious, visible projects. Preferred Qualifications Graduate Degree in Journalism, English, Communications or a related field Experience editing an alumni, institutional, or consumer magazine. Familiarity with digital publishing platforms and multimedia storytelling formats. Experience working with Advancement or Admissions to develop donor- or enrollment-facing publications. Additional Requirement for Applicants Please include writing samples that demonstrate your writing and editorial experience. Samples may include published or professional work that reflects your ability to shape content, voice, and messaging for different audiences. Compensation In addition to a competitive benefits package, annualized pay for this role is typically between $86,593.05 - $117,155.30 covering the middle half of market compensation. Actual compensation will vary based on individual qualifications and work schedule if less than full-time. Position Type Regular Please attach BOTH a current resume and a cover letter in order for your application to be considered for this position . Be sure you have provided all attachments before submitting your application. You will NOT be able to attach additional files after you have hit the Submit button. Review of applications will begin October 22, 2026 About Smith College Located in Northampton, MA, Smith College is one of the largest women’s colleges in the country and is dedicated to excellence in teaching and research across the liberal arts.  A faculty of outstanding scholars interact with students in small classes, as advisors, and through student-faculty research projects. The College is a member of the Five College Consortium with Amherst, Hampshire and Mt. Holyoke Colleges, and the University of Massachusetts Amherst.  Students cross-enroll and faculty cross-teach across the Five Colleges. Consistent with the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) and Massachusetts General Law, Chapter 151B, it is the policy of Smith College to provide reasonable accommodation when requested by a qualified applicant or employee with a disability, unless such accommodation would cause an undue hardship. The policy regarding requests for reasonable accommodation applies to all aspects of employment, including the application process. If reasonable accommodation is needed, please contact our recruiting team at recruiting-u@smith.edu . As set forth in our mission and values , Smith College is committed to promoting a culture of  equity and inclusion among students, staff and faculty.  The College will not discriminate in employment on the basis of age, race, color, ethnicity, national origin, creed, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, pregnancy, genetic information, age, veteran status, physical or mental disability, or any other classification protected by law. Smith College is an equal opportunity employer and complies with all state and federal laws that prohibit discrimination."
    },
    "https://smithcollege.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/smithcollege/job/Smith-College/Strategic-Communications-Writer_R-202600608": {
      "title": "Strategic Communications Writer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-28\nPosted: Posted 16 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nIf you have any questions about the position or our application process, reach out to us at recruiting-u@smith.edu . Department Communications and Marketing Job Description Job Summary The Strategic Communications Writer serves as Smith College's presidential writer and executive communications strategist. This position is responsible for shaping and articulating the voice of the president across speeches, remarks, correspondence, opinion pieces, campus communications, and other executive messaging to internal and external audiences. Working at the intersection of strategy, leadership, and storytelling, the Strategic Communications Writer helps translate the president's vision, the college's priorities, and complex institutional issues into compelling communications that engage internal and external audiences and advance Smith's mission and reputation. Reporting to the Senior Director of Media Relations and Strategic Communications, this position works in close partnership with the President and senior leaders across the college—including the Offices of the President, Advancement, Enrollment, Government Relations, and General Counsel—to ensure presidential communications are accurate, thoughtful, timely, and aligned with institutional priorities. This position identifies opportunities to advance institutional priorities and develops integrated messaging strategies that support the President’s leadership across multiple audiences and platforms. Success in this role requires exceptional writing and editing skills, sound judgment, political acumen, intellectual curiosity, and the ability to synthesize complex information into persuasive narratives under tight deadlines. Through speeches, talking points, public remarks, and executive communications, this individual plays a central role in advancing the president's leadership and strengthening Smith College's relationships with students, faculty, staff, alums, donors, government officials, and other key stakeholders. Take appropriate actions to support a diverse workforce and participate in the College's efforts to create a respectful, inclusive, and welcoming work environment. Essential Functions Serve as the primary communications writer for the President, developing speeches, remarks, correspondence, talking points, opinion pieces, and other executive communications. Translate complex institutional priorities, policies, and strategic initiatives into clear, compelling communications for diverse audiences. Ensure consistency and authenticity of the President's voice across all communications and public appearances. Conduct research, interviews, and background preparation to ensure executive communications are accurate, nuanced, and well-informed. Work with the presidential speech and writing calendar, coordinating research, review, revisions, approvals, and logistics while maintaining strict confidentiality. Work closely with the Senior Director of Media Relations and Strategic Communications to ensure alignment between presidential communications, institutional messaging, and media strategy. Collaborate with Communications and Marketing colleagues and campus partners to identify opportunities to elevate the President's thought leadership and strengthen Smith's public profile. Advise the President and senior leaders on messaging strategy, framing, and communications approaches for significant institutional issues and initiatives. Develop messaging frameworks and key messages that support institutional priorities and can be adapted across multiple communications channels. Prepare the President for speeches, interviews, meetings, panels, and media opportunities through briefing materials and talking points. Monitor higher education, public policy, and current events to identify emerging issues and opportunities that may affect presidential communications. Maintain an archive of presidential speeches and messaging resources to support consistency and institutional memory. Evaluate the effectiveness of executive communications and recommend improvements based on audience feedback and institutional priorities. Perform other duties as assigned. MINIMUM REQUIRED Qualifications (education, experience, certifications, licenses, knowledge, skills) Bachelor's Degree in Communications, Journalism, English, Political Science, Public Policy or a related field 8 years of experience in executive communications, journalism, public affairs or a related field Demonstrated experience writing for senior executives or organizational leaders. Demonstrated ability to manage multiple complex projects with competing deadlines. Skills Exceptional writing, editing, and verbal communication skills, with the ability to synthesize complex ideas into compelling narratives while maintaining a consistent executive voice. Strong political judgment, discretion, and ability to handle confidential information. Exceptional interpersonal skills with the ability to build credibility and influence at all organizational levels. Preferred Qualifications Experience in higher education, government, philanthropy, or another complex mission-driven organization. Experience supporting presidents, CEOs, elected officials, or other highly visible leaders. Familiarity with AP Style and higher education communications. Experience developing communications around complex policy, governance, or institutional issues. Strategic thinker with strong editorial judgment and intellectual curiosity who quickly masters unfamiliar subjects. Ability to build trusted relationships with senior leaders and collaborate effectively across a decentralized organization. Demonstrated ability to write with warmth, wit, and emotional intelligence when appropriate. Additional Requirement for Applicants Please include writing samples that demonstrate your ability to develop clear, compelling content for different audiences and purposes. Samples may include published or professional work. Compensation In addition to a competitive benefits package, annualized pay for this role is typically between $80,410.25 - $108,790.34 covering the middle half of market compensation. Actual compensation will vary based on individual qualifications and work schedule if less than full-time. Position Type Regular Please attach BOTH a current resume and a cover letter in order for your application to be considered for this position . Be sure you have provided all attachments before submitting your application. You will NOT be able to attach additional files after you have hit the Submit button. Review of applications will begin October 22, 2026 About Smith College Located in Northampton, MA, Smith College is one of the largest women’s colleges in the country and is dedicated to excellence in teaching and research across the liberal arts.  A faculty of outstanding scholars interact with students in small classes, as advisors, and through student-faculty research projects. The College is a member of the Five College Consortium with Amherst, Hampshire and Mt. Holyoke Colleges, and the University of Massachusetts Amherst.  Students cross-enroll and faculty cross-teach across the Five Colleges. Consistent with the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) and Massachusetts General Law, Chapter 151B, it is the policy of Smith College to provide reasonable accommodation when requested by a qualified applicant or employee with a disability, unless such accommodation would cause an undue hardship. The policy regarding requests for reasonable accommodation applies to all aspects of employment, including the application process. If reasonable accommodation is needed, please contact our recruiting team at recruiting-u@smith.edu . As set forth in our mission and values , Smith College is committed to promoting a culture of  equity and inclusion among students, staff and faculty.  The College will not discriminate in employment on the basis of age, race, color, ethnicity, national origin, creed, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, pregnancy, genetic information, age, veteran status, physical or mental disability, or any other classification protected by law. Smith College is an equal opportunity employer and complies with all state and federal laws that prohibit discrimination."
    },
    "https://smithcollege.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/smithcollege/job/Smith-College/Senior-Operations-Coordinator--Alumnae-Engagement---Volunteer-Leadership_R-202600610": {
      "title": "Senior Operations Coordinator, Alumnae Engagement & Volunteer Leadership",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-25\nPosted: Posted 19 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nIf you have any questions about the position or our application process, reach out to us at recruiting-u@smith.edu . Department Alumnae Relations & Development Job Description Job Summary Provide comprehensive administrative support to the AVP of Alumnae Relations/ Executive Director of the AASC. Lead the administrative planning and activity/event coordination associated with the Alumnae Association of Smith College (AASC) Board of Directors + their subcommittees (Nominating Committee) and the Medal Committee, two of ARD’s primary Leadership volunteer groups. In support of the AASC Executive Director, plan, prepare for and implement all virtual and in-person meetings of the AASC Board of Directors and its subcommittees, and the Medal Committee. Prepare and/or edit relevant documentation such as rosters, meeting agendas and other support materials. Maintain shared drive organization in support of alum leadership groups and staff stakeholders. Support alums in these groups with their needs and travel plans to campus, or to ambassadorship opportunities.   Serve as the administrative liaison between the President’s Office and the Office of Alumnae Relations with regard to all OAR programming in which the President participates (including the AASC board meetings, class reunion events, Ivy Day and the Alumnae Gathering.) Serve as the tactical lead on team-wide faculty stewardship and AASC-originated volunteer stewardship. Support general administration for the team as needed including implementation of programming, management of data, prepar ation of materials, event logistics, financial reconciliation, and other projects as assigned. Take appropriate actions to support a diverse workforce and participate in the College's efforts to create a respectful, inclusive, and welcoming work environment. Essential Functions Internal Administrative Support Seasonal support of reunion-related administration. Liaise with the Executive Assistant for Advancement to ensure continuity in the administration of details related to VIP constituencies and the President’s Office. Work with the Executive Director and other members of the alumnae relations team to provide strategic administrative support in the planning and implementation of events and programs both on and off-campus and virtually. Work with team members across OAR as the administrative liaison between the President’s Office and the Office of Alumnae Relations with regard to all OAR programming in which the President participates (including the AASC board meetings, class reunion events, Ivy Day and the Alumnae Gathering.) Provide tactical leadership on administration related to faculty stewardship and AASC-originated volunteer stewardship. Facilitate communication and partnerships between members of the Alumnae Relations team and other Alumnae Relations & Development staff who support AR projects via email communication, website maintenance, data and reporting, and virtual meeting formats. Conduct or delegate key administrative tasks including, but not limited to:  provide on-site logistical coordination and staffing for events. Schedule events, rooms and catering through 25Live; coordinate travel and logistics for staff and alumnae; research and reserve venues and off-site catering; coordinate materials prep for conferences and career events; approve alumnae for LinkedIn groups; organize and lead webinars; process A/P vouchers and reconcile payments. Work with the Executive Director to track and manage budget and expenses within the VP cost center and the AASC Board cost center; support the collection of budgetary updates from across the AR team. External Administrative Support Proactively support members of the AASC Board of Directors. Support their successful communication, board culture and function by performing strategic administrative tasks at a high level. Maintain communications with the board, produce regular messages including digests of campus news and communications, support board members and sub-committees with their administrative needs.  Work with the Executive Director to support volunteer leaders and their meetings by preparing agendas, preparing speakers, producing pre-meeting and in-meeting materials and resources. Anticipate and arrange long-term logistical needs of the board by planning for hotel accommodations and space reservations required more than one year in advance. Coordinate travel, accommodations and/or video conferencing arrangements for directors’ participation in meetings. Maintain official records of leadership volunteer meetings, including minutes and reports. Create and maintain accurate contact records (addresses, employment, etc.) and bios for all current and former directors in the database and in Board materials. Monitor related budgets; process all reimbursement vouchers and invoices charged to the board budget. Regular phone and email contact with the president, officers and committees. Respond to board member requests. Assist with the organization and scheduling of meetings as needed, including the preparation of all meeting materials and  information. Maintain yearly AASC, Nominating Committee a nd Medal Committee calendars and support the Executive Director to fulfill related deliverables (quarterly budget reports, trustee reports.) Reunion Student Program Support the Assistant Director of Alumnae Leadership and Career Engagement with administrative tasks related to the Reunion Student Program including budget management and financial reconciliation, student payroll, space reservation, account administration of the scheduling platform, update training materials, tabling and program promotion, email communication to potential applicants, ordering of all supplies, coordination of deliveries and setup of the Reunion Student Program headquarters. Serve as one of two primary supervisors of RSP during the two-week run of the Reunion Student Program; Supervise student leads in the RSP program. Other Duties Participate in team meetings, task forces and committees as required. Back up other staff needs as necessary. Perform related duties as required. All employees are expected to participate in the College's efforts to create a respectful, inclusive, and welcoming work environment. MINIMUM REQUIRED Qualifications (education, experience, certifications, licenses, knowledge, skills) Associate's Degree and 1-5 years experience and/or an equivalent combination of education and expereince Must possess strong organizational skills and strong attention to detail Be able to prioritize work effectively, take initiative and solve problems. Strong technology skills. Ability to maintain strict confidentiality, exercise diplomacy and good judgment with all college constituents. Able to provide excellent customer service while interacting effectively with a diverse population including alumnae, students, faculty, staff and outside vendors. Ability to work collaboratively as a team member and to manage projects and workload independently. Strong commitment to accuracy Excellent interpersonal, communication skills and professional demeanor. Commitment to professional development and cultural values. Preferred Qualifications Higher Education, Volunteer support NOTE:  this position requires willingness to work occasional overtime with evenings and weekends (approximately 5 weekends per year). #ALMDEV# Experience with Microsoft, Google Suite, Salesforce, and Zoom is preferred. Compensation In addition to a competitive benefits package, pay for this role is typically between $27.17 - $33.21 hourly covering the middle half of market compensation. Actual compensation will vary based on individual qualifications. Position Type Regular Please attach BOTH a current resume and a cover letter in order for your application to be considered for this position . Be sure you have provided all attachments before submitting your application. You will NOT be able to attach additional files after you have hit the Submit button. Review of applications will begin October 16, 2026 About Smith College Located in Northampton, MA, Smith College is one of the largest women’s colleges in the country and is dedicated to excellence in teaching and research across the liberal arts.  A faculty of outstanding scholars interact with students in small classes, as advisors, and through student-faculty research projects. The College is a member of the Five College Consortium with Amherst, Hampshire and Mt. Holyoke Colleges, and the University of Massachusetts Amherst.  Students cross-enroll and faculty cross-teach across the Five Colleges. Consistent with the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) and Massachusetts General Law, Chapter 151B, it is the policy of Smith College to provide reasonable accommodation when requested by a qualified applicant or employee with a disability, unless such accommodation would cause an undue hardship. The policy regarding requests for reasonable accommodation applies to all aspects of employment, including the application process. If reasonable accommodation is needed, please contact our recruiting team at recruiting-u@smith.edu . As set forth in our mission and values , Smith College is committed to promoting a culture of  equity and inclusion among students, staff and faculty.  The College will not discriminate in employment on the basis of age, race, color, ethnicity, national origin, creed, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, pregnancy, genetic information, age, veteran status, physical or mental disability, or any other classification protected by law. Smith College is an equal opportunity employer and complies with all state and federal laws that prohibit discrimination."
    },
    "https://smithcollege.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/smithcollege/job/Smith-College/Evening-Custodian-Anticipated-Openings_R-202600598": {
      "title": "Evening Custodian Anticipated Openings",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-21\nPosted: Posted 23 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nIf you have any questions about the position or our application process, reach out to us at recruiting-u@smith.edu . Job Description PRIMARY FUNCTION(S):  To provide a safe and sanitary environment with other necessary support for routine and special activities required of Facilities Management building services.    Work shift is M-F, 3:30pm-11:30pm. DUTIES: Clean rest rooms: toilets, urinals, mirrors, sinks, windows and glass, walls, floors, fixtures, restock paper goods, soap and re-fill sani-machines. Dry mop, wet mop, buff, strip and refinish floors. Clean all spaces within assigned areas (i.e., offices, classrooms, labs, storage areas, corridors, custodial closets, etc.) Vacuum carpets, walk-off mats and runners in all assigned areas. Remove leaves, snow and debris from all entrances of assigned areas. Perform emergency clean-up of floods, spills and breakage within the building, excluding hazardous materials. Perform non-technical tasks as assigned to include, but not limited to changing light bulbs, filling/cleaning humidifiers, cleaning drinking fountains, emptying dehumidifiers, changing screens, opening and closing buildings. Check and clean eye wash stations, emergency showers and fire extinguishers according to maintenance schedule. Report all necessary maintenance and repair items to the coordinator, supervisor or designee. Conduct daily building inspections for possible safety hazards and report findings to the supervisor. Maintain positive public relations with all members of the college community. Perform tasks for scheduled and unscheduled academic, community events and other activities as required. Clean, sweep and mop all stairwells, including assigned floor and one flight up, unless otherwise noted. Requires trash removal, recycling and other materials from buildings. Must comply with cleaning frequency requirements as scheduled for assigned areas. Perform other related duties as assigned.   MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: A driver’s license is not required for this position, however, if the employee accepts driving duties, they must complete and comply with all college driving credentialing requirements, which includes a Division of Motor Vehicles background check. Must be able to safely lift and carry up to fifty pounds. Must be able to bend, walk, stand, climb stairs and ladders, and safely operate loaded hand trucks and dollies.   Education/Experience: High school/vocational diploma, GED equivalent or combination of education and experience. Minimum of 1 year (preferred) custodial/housekeeping experience in a service related industry.   Skills: Ability to move, deliver and arrange heavy items such as equipment, furniture, fixtures and materials within and between buildings for students, faculty, staff and guests of the college. Know the cleaning schedule for all assigned areas and look for ways to improve on that schedule. Know and have a basic understanding of the chemicals and processes used in the cleaning schedule; be familiar with chemical safety and Safety Data Sheets (SDS). Ability to remove ice and snow from entrances, walkways, ramps, fire escapes, porches and emergency exits attached or leading to buildings, which includes shoveling, scraping and sanding. Ability to use good judgment to take appropriate, independent action to carry out the primary functions of the job. Must have general computer knowledge. Compensation This position is covered by a collective bargaining agreement with a progressive wage schedule. The Step 1 hourly rate is $24.52. New hires are typically placed at Step 1. Position Type Regular Please attach BOTH a current resume and a cover letter in order for your application to be considered for this position . Be sure you have provided all attachments before submitting your application. You will NOT be able to attach additional files after you have hit the Submit button. About Smith College Located in Northampton, MA, Smith College is one of the largest women’s colleges in the country and is dedicated to excellence in teaching and research across the liberal arts.  A faculty of outstanding scholars interact with students in small classes, as advisors, and through student-faculty research projects. The College is a member of the Five College Consortium with Amherst, Hampshire and Mt. Holyoke Colleges, and the University of Massachusetts Amherst. Students cross-enroll and faculty cross-teach across the Five Colleges. Consistent with the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) and Massachusetts General Law, Chapter 151B, it is the policy of Smith College to provide reasonable accommodation when requested by a qualified applicant or employee with a disability, unless such accommodation would cause an undue hardship. The policy regarding requests for reasonable accommodation applies to all aspects of employment, including the application process. If reasonable accommodation is needed, please contact our recruiting team at recruiting-u@smith.edu . As set forth in our mission and values , Smith College is committed to promoting a culture of  equity and inclusion among students, staff and faculty.  The College will not discriminate in employment on the basis of age, race, color, ethnicity, national origin, creed, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, pregnancy, genetic information, age, veteran status, physical or mental disability, or any other classification protected by law. Smith College is an equal opportunity employer and complies with all state and federal laws that prohibit discrimination."
    },
    "https://smithcollege.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/smithcollege/job/Smith-College/Gardener-and-Assistant-Curator_R-202600573": {
      "title": "Gardener and Assistant Curator",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-10\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nIf you have any questions about the position or our application process, reach out to us at recruiting-u@smith.edu . Department Botanic Garden Job Description PRIMARY FUNCTIONS:  With guidance from the Landscape Curator, work independently and with other Botanic Garden staff to maintain the health, aesthetics, safety, maps, and records of the RBR Border, Rock Garden, Systematics, Fern Garden, Lanning Fountain landscape, annual border, Lyman beds, and adjacent lawns and gardens. Work with supervisor to select and or propagate plants for these areas as required and assigned.  Regularly supervise, mentor, train, collaborate with, and serve as a resource for interns, work study students, faculty, and researchers who wish to use the collections for research, education, conservation, and recreation. As needed, assist with other landscape care projects and events as assigned by the Landscape Curator or Director. Support efforts to advance inclusion and belonging in teaching and learning about plants, people, and place, including efforts to broaden access and participation in public horticulture and related educational opportunities. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES:  Independently maintain the RBR Border, Rock Garden, Systematics, Fern Garden, Lanning Fountain landscape, annual border, Lyman beds, and adjacent lawns and gardens to the highest professional horticultural standards.  Apply horticultural knowledge to maintain healthy plants through plant selection, propagation, soil management, watering, fertilization, plant placement, weeding, mowing, pest/disease treatments, and all other appropriate interventions.  Ensure garden infrastructure, structures, and hardscaping are in good repair and communicate deficiencies to appropriate personnel.   Design, plan, renovate, and install new plantings. Consult college policies, Collection Policy priorities, Collections Management Plan, Botanic Garden educational objectives, and strategic planning goals to guide selection of plant materials, design of spaces, and installation of associated infrastructure.   Maintain accurate plant records in the collections database, GIS database, and related record keeping systems. Inventory, install, and replace plant tags in garden spaces. Follow protocols for accessioning, deaccessioning, inventorying, and moving plants. Enable the work of the Manager of Living Collections through regular communication and collaboration.   Coordinate activities and projects with other Botanic Garden staff to ensure efficient and timely completion of work. Create and assess work plans that optimize collection health, campus aesthetics, safety, time management, and efficient use of resources.  Assist other gardeners, horticulturalists, and facilities staff with projects as needed.   Contribute to integrated pest management strategies and solutions. Scout for pests and diseases and maintain plantings in a way that minimizes the need for chemical interventions. Take necessary steps to manage pest and disease issues including applying pesticides in compliance with all state and federal regulations. Stay current on emerging pests and pathogens and their detection, treatments, and prevention.   Enable and support research, education, and student engagement at the Botanic Garden. Provide a supportive experiential learning environment for interns, work-study students, and those using the garden for scholarship or recreation. Supervise, mentor, train, collaborate with, and serve as a resource for students, interns, and work study students. Facilitate access to the collections, gardens, and arboretum for research, education, conservation, and recreation.  Maintain accurate inventories of supplies, tools, work spaces, and materials. Ensure that tools and work spaces are safe, secure, organized and functional. Make service requests or schedule specialist services when needed.   Communicate purchasing needs and budget requests to supervisor. Obtain estimates for products, services, and materials.  Perform duties related to garden and conservatory collection maintenance, collection management, curation, and botanic garden operations as required for the Botanic Garden to thrive.  Drive pick-up truck or utility cart when needed.  MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS:  Bachelor’s Degree in horticulture, or a related field, or equivalent professional experience. 3 to 5 years of experience working professionally in horticulture with an emphasis on perennial garden design, curation and maintenance.  Minimum of one-year experience in horticultural work with alpine plants, or equivalent formal education.  Valid driver’s license required. Must complete and comply with all College driving credentialing requirements. Upon award of this position, a Division of Motor Vehicles background check will be completed.  Obtain a Massachusetts Core Applicator or Private Applicator License within 6 months of hire.  Willingness to upgrade to at private certification if required (within one year of being notified by the college).   Physical ability to lift 40 pounds and bend and stretch with reasonable nimbleness, team lift objects up to 80 pounds, move and plant (with assistance) heavy balled trees and shrubs.  SKILLS: Experience with and capacity to support inclusive programming designed for teaching and learning about plants, people, and place, including efforts to broaden a sense of belonging, and access to and participation in public horticulture and related educational opportunities. Extensive and broad knowledge of plant identification, cultivation and propagation. Knowledge of and experience with aesthetic presentation of annuals, perennials and woody plants appropriate for gardens in the local climate. A test will be given to confirm plant identification and horticultural skills.   Demonstrated ability to design garden beds and implement installations. Ability to understand written and verbal instructions.   Ability to use horticultural chemicals, hand tools, general horticultural equipment, machinery, power tools (including a chainsaw) and materials safely and effectively.   Ability to independently make sound and correct decisions on the care of garden plants and shrubs.   Ability to maintain accurate records, maps, and plant labels using Botanic Garden software, computer equipment, and printing/engraving equipment.  Ability to send and receive email in a timely manner.  Willingness and ability to adapt to modern technology, methodologies, and updates in the scientific literature.   Ability to work with others as a team to complete priority projects as they arise. Ability to supervise student workers, interns, and other labor.  Ability to correctly identify regionally common perennial, tree, and shrub species, pests and pathogens, and correctly evaluate plant health and safety. A verbal test on campus will be given. Must demonstrate proficiency in accessing the internet, using the Smith College email and calendar systems, Workday (payroll & HR system) and applicable work order system(s).  Experience using IrisBG preferred, or willingness and ability to learn and utilize the system otherwise. Capacity and willingness to participate in continuing education and on-the-job training.  WORKING CONDITIONS:  Exposure to all types of outside weather conditions and diverse horticultural plant materials. Occasional work with pesticides and other horticultural chemicals. Schedule: Mon.-Fri., 7:00 a.m. – 3:00 p.m.  Applications will continue being reviewed on a rolling basis until the positions is filled. Compensation This position is covered by a collective bargaining agreement with a progressive wage schedule. The Step 1 hourly rate is $28.77. New hires are typically placed at Step 1. Position Type Regular Please attach BOTH a current resume and a cover letter in order for your application to be considered for this position . Be sure you have provided all attachments before submitting your application. You will NOT be able to attach additional files after you have hit the Submit button. Review of applications will begin October 22, 2026 About Smith College Located in Northampton, MA, Smith College is one of the largest women’s colleges in the country and is dedicated to excellence in teaching and research across the liberal arts.  A faculty of outstanding scholars interact with students in small classes, as advisors, and through student-faculty research projects. The College is a member of the Five College Consortium with Amherst, Hampshire and Mt. Holyoke Colleges, and the University of Massachusetts Amherst.  Students cross-enroll and faculty cross-teach across the Five Colleges. Consistent with the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) and Massachusetts General Law, Chapter 151B, it is the policy of Smith College to provide reasonable accommodation when requested by a qualified applicant or employee with a disability, unless such accommodation would cause an undue hardship. The policy regarding requests for reasonable accommodation applies to all aspects of employment, including the application process. If reasonable accommodation is needed, please contact our recruiting team at recruiting-u@smith.edu . As set forth in our mission and values , Smith College is committed to promoting a culture of  equity and inclusion among students, staff and faculty.  The College will not discriminate in employment on the basis of age, race, color, ethnicity, national origin, creed, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, pregnancy, genetic information, age, veteran status, physical or mental disability, or any other classification protected by law. Smith College is an equal opportunity employer and complies with all state and federal laws that prohibit discrimination."
    },
    "https://smithcollege.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/smithcollege/job/Smith-College/Campus-Safety-Officer--anticipated-openings-_R-202600271": {
      "title": "Campus Safety Security Member (anticipated openings)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-09\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nIf you have any questions about the position or our application process, reach out to us at recruiting-u@smith.edu . Department Campus Safety Job Description Job Summary  College security officers is a non-sworn position that assists College Police to ensure the safety of students, staff, and property through regular foot/vehicle/bike patrols, building access control, and incident response. This department is a 24/7 operation with staffing on all three shifts. Responsibilities include enforcing safety regulations, providing 24/7 security escorts, building lockouts and lockups, parking enforcement, preparing incident reports, reporting safety concerns and providing customer service. Will utilize Computer-Aided Dispatch (CAD) to properly log calls. May need to use the college’s mass notification RAVE system. This position is an entry point to pursuing a career in law enforcement/campus safety at Smith College. This is a casual, non-benefitted position. Pay for this role is typically between $21.23-$25.94 hourly covering the middle half of market compensation. Actual compensation will vary based on individual qualifications. Key Responsibilities Patrol and Inspection: Patrolling campus grounds and buildings (residential, academic, and administrative) to identify safety hazards, unauthorized persons, or breaches of security. Access Control: Locking and unlocking buildings/rooms. Emergency Response: Responding to assist Police with fire alarms, medical emergencies and disturbances, often including administering first aid or CPR. Reporting: Preparing all types of incident reports. Traffic and Safety: Directing traffic, enforcing parking regulations, and assisting motorists. Monitoring Systems: Radio and alarm Public Interaction: Handle walk-in requests for assistance at the police department.  Required Skills and Qualifications Physical & Schedule needs: Frequently requires working nights, weekends, and holidays, along with the ability to walk for extended periods and stand for long durations. Communication: Excellent verbal and written skills, with the ability to maintain composure during stressful, high-pressure situations. Technical Proficiency: Ability to operate radio systems, CAD software, and Microsoft Office. Be able to attain DCJIS certification. Certifications: While not required - EMD (Emergency Medical Dispatch) and APCO PST1 certifications, or the ability to obtain them is helpful. Background Check: Must pass a thorough background check and possess a clean drivers’ history Availability: Must be able to work varied shifts, including nights, weekends, and holidays as this position is for an on-call member (casual position with Smith College). Minimum Qualifications  High School Diploma or equivalent required. Previous experience in public safety, security, or customer service. Preferred Qualifications College Degree in law enforcement/public safety Good customer service skills Ability to work in a fast paced, multi-tasked environment  CPR/1 st Aid or ability to attain CAD knowledge  DCJIS certification Position Type Fixed Term Please attach BOTH a current resume and a cover letter in order for your application to be considered for this position. Be sure you have provided all attachments before submitting your application. You will NOT be able to attach additional files after you have hit the Submit button. Review of applications will begin About Smith College Located in Northampton, MA, Smith College is one of the largest women’s colleges in the country and is dedicated to excellence in teaching and research across the liberal arts.  A faculty of outstanding scholars interact with students in small classes, as advisors, and through student-faculty research projects. The College is a member of the Five College Consortium with Amherst, Hampshire and Mt. Holyoke Colleges, and the University of Massachusetts Amherst. Students cross-enroll and faculty cross-teach across the Five Colleges. Consistent with the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) and Massachusetts General Law, Chapter 151B, it is the policy of Smith College to provide reasonable accommodation when requested by a qualified applicant or employee with a disability, unless such accommodation would cause an undue hardship. The policy regarding requests for reasonable accommodation applies to all aspects of employment, including the application process. If reasonable accommodation is needed, please contact our recruiting team at recruiting-u@smith.edu . As set forth in our mission and values , Smith College is committed to promoting a culture of  equity and inclusion among students, staff and faculty.  The College will not discriminate in employment on the basis of age, race, color, ethnicity, national origin, creed, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, pregnancy, genetic information, age, veteran status, physical or mental disability, or any other classification protected by law. Smith College is an equal opportunity employer and complies with all state and federal laws that prohibit discrimination."
    },
    "https://smithcollege.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/smithcollege/job/Smith-College/Cook-s-Assistant_R-202600561": {
      "title": "Cook's Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-08\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nIf you have any questions about the position or our application process, reach out to us at recruiting-u@smith.edu . Department Dining Services Job Description Position Summary Assist with all food preparation and service during assigned shifts.  Work cooperatively in a safe and sanitary manner with other staff members to ensure the efficient and professional operation of the unit in according with work rules and departmental policies.  Strive for a dining atmosphere which is cordial and accommodating for all students and guests under the guidance of the chef or cook on duty and direction of the area manager.  Clean all dining service areas and equipment as delegated or in concert with team members.  Assume dining room coordinator’s duties during key periods of service.  Respond with flexibility to change in work location when the operational needs of the department so required. Duties and Responsibilities: Assist the cooks, and assume the duties of the Kitchen Assistant in their absences. Prepare, cook, garnish, and present all menu items in a superior manner in accordance with dining service standards and recipes.  These include, but are not limited to breakfast items, soups, entrees including meats, sauces, vegetables, salads, dressings, breads, desserts, and meal alternatives as well as special diets/dietary accommodations and display cooking. Clean and sanitize all dining service areas and equipment including routine floor maintenance on each shift.  Support and maintain proper sanitation and recycling procedure. Perform duties of dining room coordinator as needed, including use of the dining access system and all other related tasks.  Assist in receiving:  check in product, inspect, weigh/count and properly store all food and supplies.  Assist chef in accurately inventorying storage areas.  Work with other members of kitchen staff to orderly assemble production records, transfers, and meal counts for data entry.  Communicate with all people accurately, professionally and in a timely manner, including proper labeling and dating all prepared foods.  Work cooperatively with the area manager, staff, and students to ensure an orderly and efficient operation.  Responsible for reviewing all department notices.  Utilize email and all college computer access responsibly. Attend departmental meetings and training workshops as requested.  Participate in the preparation for and service of special events.  Complete required payroll forms and scheduling forms within deadlines.  Perform all other related or relevant tasks.   Initiate teamwork, collaboration, and cooperation between co-workers. ​Minimum Qualifications: Completion of high school education or vocational school education and a minimum 1 year of combined educational and/or commercial experience in quantity cookery which includes baking. Requirements of the position are consistent with the responsibilities and duties of persons within the food service profession in adherence with all state and federal guidelines. Ability to communicate effectively both verbally and in writing and perform basic arithmetic functions. Current state approved sanitation certificate is required. Culinary arts degree or equivalent training preferred.   Skills: Ability to tolerate exposure to water and cleaning chemicals, higher than normal noise levels, and heat and humidity consistent with that produced by commercial kitchen equipment. Must demonstrate adequate physical strength and coordination to effectively prepare, clean and receive supplies while properly utilizing standard food service utensils and equipment.  Must be able to safely lift and move 50 pounds, work from a step stool, walk and stand for prolonged periods of time, bend, reach, pull and push.  Must be able to manipulate utensils and equipment repetitively for variable periods of time - up to 65% of an assigned shift. Must be mobile between work stations and other campus food service areas. Must possess good vision with or without correction. Must possess the sensory ability to taste/smell. Must practice good personal hygiene and wear clean uniforms in compliance with public health standards and consistent with college policy. Must adhere to the sustainability initiatives set by the department. Must possess basic computer skills including receiving and sending emails. Compensation This position is covered by a collective bargaining agreement with a progressive wage schedule. The Step 1 hourly rate is $25.29. New hires are typically placed at Step 1. Position Type Regular Please attach BOTH a current resume and a cover letter in order for your application to be considered for this position . Be sure you have provided all attachments before submitting your application. You will NOT be able to attach additional files after you have hit the Submit button. Review of applications will begin September 22, 2026 About Smith College Located in Northampton, MA, Smith College is one of the largest women’s colleges in the country and is dedicated to excellence in teaching and research across the liberal arts.  A faculty of outstanding scholars interact with students in small classes, as advisors, and through student-faculty research projects. The College is a member of the Five College Consortium with Amherst, Hampshire and Mt. Holyoke Colleges, and the University of Massachusetts Amherst.  Students cross-enroll and faculty cross-teach across the Five Colleges. Consistent with the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) and Massachusetts General Law, Chapter 151B, it is the policy of Smith College to provide reasonable accommodation when requested by a qualified applicant or employee with a disability, unless such accommodation would cause an undue hardship. The policy regarding requests for reasonable accommodation applies to all aspects of employment, including the application process. If reasonable accommodation is needed, please contact our recruiting team at recruiting-u@smith.edu . As set forth in our mission and values , Smith College is committed to promoting a culture of  equity and inclusion among students, staff and faculty.  The College will not discriminate in employment on the basis of age, race, color, ethnicity, national origin, creed, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, pregnancy, genetic information, age, veteran status, physical or mental disability, or any other classification protected by law. Smith College is an equal opportunity employer and complies with all state and federal laws that prohibit discrimination."
    },
    "https://smithcollege.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/smithcollege/job/Smith-College/Triple-Unit-Cook_R-202600560": {
      "title": "Triple Unit Cook",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-08\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nIf you have any questions about the position or our application process, reach out to us at recruiting-u@smith.edu . Department Dining Services Job Description Position Summary Work under the direction of the unit chef and Area manager to prepare and present meals and all related items during assigned shifts.  Work cooperatively in a safe and sanitary manner with other staff members to ensure the efficient and professional operation of the unit in accordance with departmental policies.  Review upcoming menus and food orders and work cooperatively and accurately in planning food production. The schedule is as follows: Thu: 11:30am-7:30pm Fri:  11:30am-7:30pm Sat:  11:30am-7:30pm Sun:  11:30am-7:30pm Mon: 6am-2pm Tue: off Wed: off Duties and Responsibilities: Prepare, cook, garnish, and present all menu items in a superior manner in accordance with Dining Services standards and recipes.  These include, but are not limited to, breakfast items, soups, entrees including meats, sauces, vegetables, salads, dressings, breads, desserts, and meal alternatives as well as special diets/dietary accommodations.  Assist in the preparation and service of any other meal when on duty.  Contribute to the food production demands of other shifts including tea preparation.  Check all food for proper temperature, flavor, and appearance before each meal. Monitor the food service area prior to and during service and replenish food items. Work cooperatively with Dining Room Coordinators to ensure that all food is being served in a safe and attractive manner. Utilize and properly store leftover food to ensure safe product handling and effective food cost control. Plan usages of frozen items to ensure proper thawing under refrigeration. Communicate amounts of product used to the Chef.   Understand and accurately use all available tools to control costs. Ensure that deliveries are checked in, inspected, weighed, and properly put away using first in, first out rules. Rotate other refrigerated food as needed, report any discrepancies of itemization or unacceptable quality to the Area Manager or Food Purchasing Office immediately.  Assist with the ordering of food and other supplies as necessary. Assist in the preparation and service of any other meal when on duty. Contribute to the food production demands of other shifts including tea preparation, and perform all other related or relevant tasks. Clean and sanitize all Dining Services areas and equipment including routine floor maintenance on each shift.  Support and maintain proper sanitation and recycling procedures. Communicate with all people accurately, professionally, and in a timely manner, including proper labeling and dating all prepared foods.  Work cooperatively with the area manager, staff, and students to ensure an orderly and efficient operation.  Responsible for reviewing and understanding all departmental notices.  Utilize e-mail and all college computer access responsibly.  Attend departmental meetings and training workshops as requested. Participate in the preparation and service of special events, as needed.  Complete required payroll forms and scheduling forms within deadlines. Perform all other job related or relevant tasks.  Initiate teamwork, collaboration, and cooperation between co-workers. Minimum Qualifications: Completion of high school education or vocational school education plus a minimum of two years of educational and or commercial experience in quantity cookery that includes baking.   Ability to communicate effectively both verbally and in writing and perform basic arithmetic functions. Current state approved sanitation certificate is required. Culinary Arts Degree at a post-secondary level preferred. Requirements of the position are consistent with the responsibilities and duties of persons within the food service profession in adherence with all state and federal guidelines.  Skills:   Ability to tolerate exposure to water and cleaning chemicals, higher than normal noise levels, and heat and humidity consistent with that produced by commercial kitchen equipment. Must demonstrate adequate physical strength and coordination to effectively cook, clean and receive supplies while properly utilizing standard food service utensils and equipment.  Must be able to safely lift and move 50 pounds, work from a step stool, walk and stand for prolonged periods of time, bend, reach, pull and push.  Must also be able to manipulate utensils and equipment repetitively for variable periods of time - up to 65% of an assigned shift. Must be mobile between kitchen and dining areas and other campus food service areas. Must possess the sensory ability to taste/smell. Must possess good vision with or without correction. Must practice good personal hygiene and wear clean uniforms in compliance with public health standard and consistent with college policy. Must adhere to the sustainability initiatives set by Dining Services. Compensation This position is covered by a collective bargaining agreement with a progressive wage schedule. The Step 1 hourly rate is $25.87. New hires are typically placed at Step 1. Position Type Regular Please attach BOTH a current resume and a cover letter in order for your application to be considered for this position . Be sure you have provided all attachments before submitting your application. You will NOT be able to attach additional files after you have hit the Submit button. Review of applications will begin September 22, 2026 About Smith College Located in Northampton, MA, Smith College is one of the largest women’s colleges in the country and is dedicated to excellence in teaching and research across the liberal arts.  A faculty of outstanding scholars interact with students in small classes, as advisors, and through student-faculty research projects. The College is a member of the Five College Consortium with Amherst, Hampshire and Mt. Holyoke Colleges, and the University of Massachusetts Amherst.  Students cross-enroll and faculty cross-teach across the Five Colleges. Consistent with the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) and Massachusetts General Law, Chapter 151B, it is the policy of Smith College to provide reasonable accommodation when requested by a qualified applicant or employee with a disability, unless such accommodation would cause an undue hardship. The policy regarding requests for reasonable accommodation applies to all aspects of employment, including the application process. If reasonable accommodation is needed, please contact our recruiting team at recruiting-u@smith.edu . As set forth in our mission and values , Smith College is committed to promoting a culture of  equity and inclusion among students, staff and faculty.  The College will not discriminate in employment on the basis of age, race, color, ethnicity, national origin, creed, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, pregnancy, genetic information, age, veteran status, physical or mental disability, or any other classification protected by law. Smith College is an equal opportunity employer and complies with all state and federal laws that prohibit discrimination."
    },
    "https://smithcollege.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/smithcollege/job/Smith-College/PhD-Faculty-Support-and-Program-Coordinator_R-202600530": {
      "title": "PhD Faculty Support and Program Coordinator",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-01\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nIf you have any questions about the position or our application process, reach out to us at recruiting-u@smith.edu . Department School for Social Work Job Description Job Summary As a member of the Faculty Support, Operations & Systems Office, this position serves as the primary operational and administrative coordinator supporting the PhD Program while also providing faculty support services to the MSW Program. The coordinator plays a critical role in ensuring the effective operation of the SSW PhD program by working with the Manager of Faculty Support and Director of the PhD Program, to navigate complex faculty, student, academic and operational processes. The position serves as an administrative partner to the PhD Director and a primary liaison to doctoral faculty, students and institutional partners. This position exercises discretion and judgment in managing program operations, faculty support systems, communications, and helps with the contracting processes and academic milestones. The coordinator independently manages multiple ongoing projects and operational workflows, including doctoral student progress tracking, assisting with the faculty contracting, and activity-pay administration, PhD Policy Committee support, course reserves, syllabus management, faculty onboarding and instructional support services. The position is expected to identify opportunities to improve existing systems and procedures and implement enhancements that strengthen faculty support, student services and program effectiveness in the PhD program. Take appropriate actions to support a diverse workforce and participate in the College's efforts to create a respectful, inclusive, and welcoming work environment. Essential Functions PhD Program Administration and Faculty Support (55%): Serve as the primary administrative and operational liaison for the PhD Director within the Office of Faculty Support, Operations & Systems. Manage and maintain doctoral student records, databases and academic progress tracking systems; monitor enrollment, degree requirements, dissertation milestones, examinations, and graduation requirements. Coordinate doctoral program milestones in partnership with the PhD Director and faculty, including qualifying examinations, dissertation proposal meetings, dissertation defenses, oral examinations and associated documentation and grade submissions to the SSW Registrar. Maintain program records and prepare institutional, accreditation and external reports, including census reporting and Survey of Earned Doctorates submissions. Serve as a point of contact for doctoral students and provide guidance regarding program processes, timelines and administrative requirements. Provide administrative support for clinical and research internships – ensuring accurate collection, tracking and distribution of required materials to support faculty oversight and student compliance. Support the PhD Director and the PhD Policy and Oversight Committee by coordinating meetings, preparing agendas and supporting materials, documenting committee decisions and maintaining governance records. Manage doctoral program communications and maintain accurate faculty- and student-facing information across relevant platforms and systems. Assist with budget monitoring and financial tracking; compile and analyze annual spending data to support planning, forecasting and fiscal oversight. Deliver logistical and technical support for dissertation defenses, oral examinations, meetings, and academic programming - including virtual and hybrid events. Provide Moodle course setup, troubleshooting, and support for CSWE competency data collection, gradebooks, and rubrics. Serve as a backup Sonia system user, providing operational support as needed to the Office of Practicum Learning. Review existing doctoral program processes and workflows and recommend operational improvements that enhance efficiency, accuracy, compliance and user experience. Faculty Contracting, Compensation, and Faculty Support Systems (20%): Under the guidance of the Manager of Faculty Support, assists with administering faculty appointment, contracting, reimbursement, and activity-pay processes for doctoral and adjunct faculty in collaboration with the leadership. Track invoices, reimbursements, honoraria and faculty compensation requests; coordinate payment processing and resolve discrepancies in collaboration with Human Resources, Payroll, and Finance. Support hiring processes by assisting with the maintenance of faculty records, hiring pools and curriculum vitae databases and oversee hiring communications, documentation tracking and onboarding logistics. Serve as a secondary point of contact for PhD faculty inquiries and maintain timely, accurate communications and documentation. Partner with campus offices to establish coordinated timelines and workflows for PhD faculty-centered events and training. Faculty Support Projects and Academic Operations (15%): Manage and support designated MSW faculty support projects including syllabus management and course reserves. Coordinate in-person and virtual MSW faculty onboarding sessions, trainings, lectures and academic events including registration management, technology support, setup, and breakdown. Attend monthly SSW faculty meetings; support agenda development, coordinate voting processes, document faculty decisions and prepare and distribute meeting minutes. Support end-of-term data collection in both the PhD and MSW programs to ensure timely and accurate submission of required materials. Administrative Support to the PhD Director (10%): Serve as administrative contact for the PhD Director - managing communications, scheduling meetings and supporting interactions with faculty, students, alumni, and external partners. Independently manage routine operational matters on behalf of the Director and exercise sound judgment in prioritizing requests and resolving routine to moderate issues. Support special projects and perform additional administrative and operational responsibilities as assigned to meet evolving needs within the PhD and MSW programs. Perform other duties as assigned by management. MINIMUM REQUIRED Qualifications: High School Diploma or Equivalent 5 or more years experience, Area: Academic operations, administration or program support Knowledge of: Strong proficiency with Google Workspace and Microsoft Office Suite; familiarity with academic operational processes. Skills: Demonstrated ability to exercise discretion and sound judgment, manage confidential information; ability to work independently, manage multiple priorities, and oversee complex workflows with high attention to detail; strong organizational, project management, and time-management skills; excellent interpersonal, relationship-building, written, and verbal communication skills; ability to work collaboratively with diverse faculty, students, staff, and campus partners; commitment to continuous improvement and SSW’s Five Guiding Principles. Preferred Qualifications: Bachelor's Degree Preferred Experience, Area: Experience supporting graduate or doctoral education programs, faculty affairs, academic operations, contracting, payroll or higher education administration preferred. Knowledge of: Familiarity with Moodle, Sonia, CourseLeaf, Workday, DocuSign, PollEverywhere, and Qualtrics preferred. Skills: Advanced system troubleshooting, process optimization, and operational enhancement tracking. Compensation In addition to a competitive benefits package, pay for this role is typically between $28.55 - $34.89 hourly covering the middle half of market compensation. Actual compensation will vary based on individual qualifications. Position Type Regular Please attach BOTH a current resume and a cover letter in order for your application to be considered for this position . Be sure you have provided all attachments before submitting your application. You will NOT be able to attach additional files after you have hit the Submit button. Review of applications will begin September 15, 2026 About Smith College Located in Northampton, MA, Smith College is one of the largest women’s colleges in the country and is dedicated to excellence in teaching and research across the liberal arts.  A faculty of outstanding scholars interact with students in small classes, as advisors, and through student-faculty research projects. The College is a member of the Five College Consortium with Amherst, Hampshire and Mt. Holyoke Colleges, and the University of Massachusetts Amherst.  Students cross-enroll and faculty cross-teach across the Five Colleges. Consistent with the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) and Massachusetts General Law, Chapter 151B, it is the policy of Smith College to provide reasonable accommodation when requested by a qualified applicant or employee with a disability, unless such accommodation would cause an undue hardship. The policy regarding requests for reasonable accommodation applies to all aspects of employment, including the application process. If reasonable accommodation is needed, please contact our recruiting team at recruiting-u@smith.edu . As set forth in our mission and values , Smith College is committed to promoting a culture of  equity and inclusion among students, staff and faculty.  The College will not discriminate in employment on the basis of age, race, color, ethnicity, national origin, creed, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, pregnancy, genetic information, age, veteran status, physical or mental disability, or any other classification protected by law. Smith College is an equal opportunity employer and complies with all state and federal laws that prohibit discrimination."
    },
    "https://smithcollege.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/smithcollege/job/Smith-College/Administrative-Assistant--Editorial-Coordinator_R-202600511": {
      "title": "Administrative Assistant, Editorial Coordinator",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-31\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nIf you have any questions about the position or our application process, reach out to us at recruiting-u@smith.edu . Department Sociology Job Description Job Summary Support the work operations of the journal Meridians: feminism, race, transnationalism Editor(s), partnering in the timely and professional production of each issue. As the Editorial Office Assistant for the journal, serve as its first point of contact with authors, reviewers and the press, representing the journal, Smith College, and the college’s academic mission with professionalism and integrity.    Take appropriate actions to support a diverse workforce and participate in the College's efforts to create a respectful, inclusive, and welcoming work environment. Essential Functions Editorial Operations (50%): Assist the Meridians Editor and the Administrative Manager in the publication of the journal. Coordinate workflow and communication between contributors, editor, guest editors, editorial board, and publisher. Coordinate all aspects of the submissions and publication process including intake, peer reviewer assignments, dispositions, auditing of Scholar One, issue content development and management, copy-editing, proof-reading, fact-checking, and copyright agreements, to ensure excellence in the quality of the publication and a high degree of professionalism in its operations. Respond to inquiries and provide general information for publishers, editorial board, guest editors, reviewers, and contributors. Perform related tasks as needed.   Communication (20%):  Support the Meridians Editor and the Administrative Manager of the Editorial Office in sustaining the flow and quality of double-anonymized peer review, timely dispositions, issue content development, issue marketing and branding, as well as communication and collaboration with the publisher.  Office-based communications, including dissemination of event notices across multiple platforms; serve as webmaster for the Meridians website, overseeing production of publicity and recruitment materials, including graphic design of event flyers and posters. Oversee the production, copyediting, and proofreading of monthly Meridians newsletter and social media content. Partner with other campus departments and offices in intercampus and alumnae communications. Administrative Support (20%): Support the Meridians Administrative Manager in the day-to-day operations and coordination of workflow of the Meridians Project, including:  Coordination of space use, including scheduling, general maintenance, catering, set up for events, and external booking requests through 25Live. Hiring, training and supervising student workers as needed, including facilitation of weekly student intern meetings, in coordination with the Administrative Manager, ensuring that events are appropriately scheduled in the shared calendar, and taking notes of items presented and decided at each meeting. Providing coordination and administrative support for programs, events, and workshops of the Meridians Project.  Coordination with other departments and programs on campus as appropriate and assist with college-wide events as necessary. Provide executive assistant support to the Editor as needed: arrange meetings, coordinate calendars, campus space, take minutes of meetings as needed; support photocopying, printing or other document publication needs for meetings; assist with provision of technical support, including supplies, equipment, and set up for events and online programming. Other Duties as Assigned (10%): Projects that support the overall direction of the journal and its integration with the college. Perform other duties as assigned by management. MINIMUM REQUIRED Qualifications: BA - Fields: WGSS, Area Studies, Race & Ethnicity, Cultural Studies, or similar Minimum of 2 years experience in publishing and/or academia Areas: Operational and transactional experience within an academic or intellectual enterprise. Experience with budget management and communications via print, web and social media. Minimum two years working with undergraduates and faculty to support their learning, teaching, and/or research preferred.  Knowledge of: Editorial Content Management Systems, preferably ScholarOne; Google Suite; Microsoft Suite; Wordpress or similar; Canva; Mailchimp; Zoom/webinar platforms; social media platforms Skills: Excellent time management and attention to detail required Preferred Qualifications: MA/MFA/pursuing Ph.D. - Fields: WGSS, Area Studies, Race & Ethnicity, Cultural Studies, or similar Knowledge of: Languages beyond English   Skills: Google Suite, Microsoft Suite, ScholarOne CMS or equivalent, Wordpress, Mailchimp, Canva Compensation In addition to a competitive benefits package, pay for this role is typically between $22.30 - $27.26 hourly covering the middle half of market compensation. Actual compensation will vary based on individual qualifications. Position Type Regular Please attach BOTH a current resume and a cover letter in order for your application to be considered for this position . Be sure you have provided all attachments before submitting your application. You will NOT be able to attach additional files after you have hit the Submit button. Review of applications will begin October 5, 2026 About Smith College Located in Northampton, MA, Smith College is one of the largest women’s colleges in the country and is dedicated to excellence in teaching and research across the liberal arts.  A faculty of outstanding scholars interact with students in small classes, as advisors, and through student-faculty research projects. The College is a member of the Five College Consortium with Amherst, Hampshire and Mt. Holyoke Colleges, and the University of Massachusetts Amherst.  Students cross-enroll and faculty cross-teach across the Five Colleges. Consistent with the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) and Massachusetts General Law, Chapter 151B, it is the policy of Smith College to provide reasonable accommodation when requested by a qualified applicant or employee with a disability, unless such accommodation would cause an undue hardship. The policy regarding requests for reasonable accommodation applies to all aspects of employment, including the application process. If reasonable accommodation is needed, please contact our recruiting team at recruiting-u@smith.edu . As set forth in our mission and values , Smith College is committed to promoting a culture of  equity and inclusion among students, staff and faculty.  The College will not discriminate in employment on the basis of age, race, color, ethnicity, national origin, creed, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, pregnancy, genetic information, age, veteran status, physical or mental disability, or any other classification protected by law. Smith College is an equal opportunity employer and complies with all state and federal laws that prohibit discrimination."
    },
    "https://smithcollege.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/smithcollege/job/Smith-College/Accommodations-Coordinator_R-202600447": {
      "title": "Accommodations Specialist",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-21\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nIf you have any questions about the position or our application process, reach out to us at recruiting-u@smith.edu . Department Accessibility Resource Center Job Description Job Summary The Accommodations Specialist supports the day-to-day administration and operations of academic, housing, dining, and other disability-related accommodations for students served by the Accessibility Resource Center (ARC). The position provides support for the accommodations processes, manages a designated caseload of students (including the graduate social work program), maintains confidential student records, and assists with data reporting and assessment. This role is responsible for organizing and triaging all incoming general office communications and managing and supporting outgoing ARC communications. Take appropriate actions to support a diverse workforce and participate in the College's efforts to create a respectful, inclusive, and welcoming work environment. Essential Functions Office Support (70%): Coordinates record and file systems for ARC (including WorkDay, Maxient, S Drive, Google Drive, Accommodate, and all other platforms utilized) and develops an end- of- year reporting and assessment Coordinate the implementation of approved note-taking accommodations including recruiting, onboarding, monitoring student note-takers, and approving timesheets Coordinates clinical/administrative requests for documentation and management of office file system. Constructs relevant templates and procedural flows within the online management system. Ensures compliance with local, state and federal guidelines (e.g. FERPA) impacting the storage and retrieval of student data. Assists in developing, updates and provides training and materials for new user orientation and refresher training. Organizes and maintains records according to Smith protocols. Coordinate incoming office communications including email, phone, and general inquiries, ensuring timely routing and response Assist with reception coverage during peak periods and staff absences Intakes and Accommodations Support (25%): Engage in the interactive process to determine accommodations including communication and meeting with students. Serve as the primary operational contact for accommodation implementation for assigned student populations, including students in the Master of Social Work program. Attends regular meetings and serves on other departmental and all-campus committees, as needed. Other Duties (5%): In cooperation with the Director of Accessibility, develops and operationalizes procedures for data and statistical collection and reporting. Complete specialized projects as defined by the Director of Accessibility and/or Smith College. MINIMUM REQUIRED Qualifications (education, experience, certifications, licenses, knowledge, skills) Bachelor's Degree and two years of related experience or the equivalent in a related field i.e. disability services, social work, education, etc. Experience working with individuals from diverse backgrounds Prior experience working in disability services in higher education Knowledge of FERPA, ADA/504, Disability-related laws and general compliance requirements. Understanding of accommodations and interactive process. Experience working with Accommodate system or disability related management system Preferred Qualifications Master’s degree in related field i.e. disability services in higher education, social work, etc Compensation In addition to a competitive benefits package, pay for this role is typically between $28.55 - $34.89 hourly covering the middle half of market compensation. Actual compensation will vary based on individual qualifications. Position Type Regular Please attach BOTH a current resume and a cover letter in order for your application to be considered for this position . Be sure you have provided all attachments before submitting your application. You will NOT be able to attach additional files after you have hit the Submit button. Review of applications will begin September 10, 2026 About Smith College Located in Northampton, MA, Smith College is one of the largest women’s colleges in the country and is dedicated to excellence in teaching and research across the liberal arts.  A faculty of outstanding scholars interact with students in small classes, as advisors, and through student-faculty research projects. The College is a member of the Five College Consortium with Amherst, Hampshire and Mt. Holyoke Colleges, and the University of Massachusetts Amherst.  Students cross-enroll and faculty cross-teach across the Five Colleges. Consistent with the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) and Massachusetts General Law, Chapter 151B, it is the policy of Smith College to provide reasonable accommodation when requested by a qualified applicant or employee with a disability, unless such accommodation would cause an undue hardship. The policy regarding requests for reasonable accommodation applies to all aspects of employment, including the application process. If reasonable accommodation is needed, please contact our recruiting team at recruiting-u@smith.edu . As set forth in our mission and values , Smith College is committed to promoting a culture of  equity and inclusion among students, staff and faculty.  The College will not discriminate in employment on the basis of age, race, color, ethnicity, national origin, creed, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, pregnancy, genetic information, age, veteran status, physical or mental disability, or any other classification protected by law. Smith College is an equal opportunity employer and complies with all state and federal laws that prohibit discrimination."
    },
    "https://smithcollege.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/smithcollege/job/Smith-College/Cook-s-Assistant_R-202600451": {
      "title": "Cook's Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-21\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nIf you have any questions about the position or our application process, reach out to us at recruiting-u@smith.edu . Department Dining Services Job Description Position Summary: Assist with all food preparation and service during assigned shifts. Work cooperatively in a safe and sanitary manner with other staff members to ensure the efficient and professional operation of the unit in according with work rules and departmental policies. Strive for a dining atmosphere which is cordial and accommodating for all students and guests under the guidance of the chef or cook on duty and direction of the area manager. Clean all dining service areas and equipment as delegated or in concert with team members. Assume dining room coordinator’s duties during key periods of service. Respond with flexibility to change in work location when the operational needs of the department so required. Duties and Responsibilities: Assist the cooks, and assume the duties of the Kitchen Assistant in their absences. Prepare, cook, garnish, and present all menu items in a superior manner in accordance with dining service standards and recipes. These include, but are not limited to breakfast items, soups, entrees including meats, sauces, vegetables, salads, dressings, breads, desserts, and meal alternatives as well as dietary accommodations and display cooking. Clean and sanitize all dining service areas and equipment including routine floor maintenance on each shift. Support and maintain proper sanitation and recycling procedure. Perform duties of dining room coordinator as needed, including use of the dining access system and all other related tasks. Assist in receiving: check in product, inspect, weigh/count and properly store all food and supplies. Assist chef in accurately inventorying storage areas. Work with other members of kitchen staff to orderly assemble production records, transfers, and meal counts for data entry. Communicate with all people accurately, professionally and in a timely manner, including proper labeling and dating all prepared foods. Work cooperatively with the area manager, staff, and students to ensure an orderly and efficient operation. Responsible for reviewing all department notices. Utilize email and all college computer access responsibly. Attend departmental meetings and training workshops as requested. Participate in the preparation for and service of special events. Complete required payroll forms and scheduling forms within deadlines. Perform all other related or relevant tasks. Initiate teamwork, collaboration, and cooperation between co-workers. Qualifications: Education/Experience: Completion of high school education or vocational school education and a minimum 1 year of combined educational and/or commercial experience in quantity cookery which includes baking. Requirements of the position are consistent with the responsibilities and duties of persons within the food service profession in adherence with all state and federal guidelines. Ability to communicate effectively both verbally and in writing and perform basic arithmetic functions. Current state approved sanitation certificate is required. Culinary arts degree or equivalent training preferred. Skills: Ability to tolerate exposure to water and cleaning chemicals, higher than normal noise levels, and heat and humidity consistent with that produced by commercial kitchen equipment. Must demonstrate adequate physical strength and coordination to effectively prepare, clean and receive supplies while properly utilizing standard food service utensils and equipment. Must be able to safely lift and move 50 pounds, work from a step stool, walk and stand for prolonged periods of time, bend, reach, pull and push. Must be able to manipulate utensils and equipment repetitively for variable periods of time - up to 65% of an assigned shift. Must be mobile between work stations and other campus food service areas. Must possess good vision with or without correction. Must possess the sensory ability to taste/smell. Must practice good personal hygiene and wear clean uniforms in compliance with public health standards and consistent with college policy. Must adhere to the sustainability initiatives set by the department. Must possess basic computer skills including receiving and sending emails. Compensation This position is covered by a collective bargaining agreement with a progressive wage schedule. The Step 1 hourly rate is $25.29. New hires are typically placed at Step 1. Position Type Regular Please attach BOTH a current resume and a cover letter in order for your application to be considered for this position . Be sure you have provided all attachments before submitting your application. You will NOT be able to attach additional files after you have hit the Submit button. Review of applications will begin September 3, 2026 About Smith College Located in Northampton, MA, Smith College is one of the largest women’s colleges in the country and is dedicated to excellence in teaching and research across the liberal arts.  A faculty of outstanding scholars interact with students in small classes, as advisors, and through student-faculty research projects. The College is a member of the Five College Consortium with Amherst, Hampshire and Mt. Holyoke Colleges, and the University of Massachusetts Amherst.  Students cross-enroll and faculty cross-teach across the Five Colleges. Consistent with the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) and Massachusetts General Law, Chapter 151B, it is the policy of Smith College to provide reasonable accommodation when requested by a qualified applicant or employee with a disability, unless such accommodation would cause an undue hardship. The policy regarding requests for reasonable accommodation applies to all aspects of employment, including the application process. If reasonable accommodation is needed, please contact our recruiting team at recruiting-u@smith.edu . As set forth in our mission and values , Smith College is committed to promoting a culture of  equity and inclusion among students, staff and faculty.  The College will not discriminate in employment on the basis of age, race, color, ethnicity, national origin, creed, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, pregnancy, genetic information, age, veteran status, physical or mental disability, or any other classification protected by law. Smith College is an equal opportunity employer and complies with all state and federal laws that prohibit discrimination."
